{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengxue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fengxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Direct Regret Optimization in Bayesian Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T04:09:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:18:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T04:00:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:36:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bayesian optimization (BO) is a powerful paradigm for optimizing expensive black-box functions. Traditional BO methods typically rely on separate hand-crafted acquisition functions and surrogate models for the underlying function, and often operate in a myopic manner. In this paper, we propose a novel direct regret optimization approach that jointly learns the optimal model and non-myopic acquisition by distilling from a set of candidate models and acquisitions, and explicitly targets minimizing the multi-step regret. Our framework leverages an ensemble of Gaussian Processes (GPs) with varying hyperparameters to generate simulated BO trajectories, each guided by an acquisition function drawn from a pool of conventional choices and terminated by a Bayesian early stop criterion. These trajectories train an end-to-end Decision Transformer that selects the next query so as to improve the ultimate objective, following a dense training sparse learning paradigm: the transformer is trained on abundant simulated data, while a limited number of real evaluations refine the GPs online. On synthetic and real-world benchmarks, our method attains the best or near-best final simple regret against standard, lookahead, trust-region and amortized BO baselines, with the largest gains in high-dimensional settings. Ablations attribute the gains jointly to region-of-interest filtering and the learned policy, and matched-budget comparisons against explicit two-step lookahead acquisitions show that the advantage is not shared by lookahead alone."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:32:38Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:32:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:14:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victoria R.","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Victoria R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny","familyName":"Kaufmann","name":"Kaufmann, Jenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Wattenberg","name":"Wattenberg, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Alvarez-Melis","name":"Alvarez-Melis, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naomi","familyName":"Saphra","name":"Saphra, Naomi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Interpretation Predict Behavior on Unseen Data?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T23:07:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:09:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-17T19:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:08:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-21T16:59:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-22T01:02:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-12T21:42:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:51:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interpretability research often predicts model responses to targeted mechanistic interventions. But can we predict responses to unseen input data? We propose and demonstrate this alternate objective by using model internals to predict their out-of-distribution (OOD) behavior. We train hundreds of Transformers on simple synthetic tasks, where perfect in-distribution accuracy is compatible with multiple OOD generalization rules. We successfully use attention patterns -- observed only on in-distribution data -- to predict which rule each model follows on OOD data. Our experiments decouple the mechanistic faithfulness of our interpretation from its predictive value; ablations reveal such internal patterns can suppress rather than support the rule they predict, showing observational analysis can forecast behavior even when causal analysis fails to support a simple cause-effect link. Our findings are a proof-of-concept for a new interpretability objective: understanding model internals to predict behavior and assess reliability under distribution shift."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:30:30Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:30:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:40:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Blesson","familyName":"Varghese","name":"Varghese, Blesson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ampere: Communication-Efficient and High-Accuracy Split Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T20:54:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:00:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T13:36:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:50:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A Federated Learning (FL) system collaboratively trains neural networks across devices and a server but is limited by significant on-device computation costs. Split Federated Learning (SFL) systems mitigate this by offloading a block of layers of the network from the device to a server. However, in doing so, it introduces large communication overheads due to frequent exchanges of intermediate activations and gradients between devices and the server and reduces model accuracy for non-IID data. We propose Ampere, a novel collaborative training system that simultaneously minimizes on-device computation and device-server communication while improving model accuracy. Unlike SFL, which uses a global loss by iterative end-to-end training, Ampere develops unidirectional inter-block training to sequentially train the device and server blocks with a local loss, eliminating the transfer of gradients. A lightweight auxiliary network generation method decouples training between the device and server, reducing frequent intermediate exchanges to a single transfer, which significantly reduces the communication overhead. Ampere mitigates the impact of data heterogeneity by consolidating activations generated by the trained device block to train the server block, in contrast to SFL, which trains on device-specific, non-IID activations. Extensive experiments on multiple CNNs and Transformers show that, compared to state-of-the-art SFL baseline systems, Ampere (i) improves model accuracy by up to 11.70 percentage points while training up to 18.6x faster, (ii) incurs up to 911x lower device-server communication overhead and up to 14.5x lower on-device computation, and (iii) reduces standard deviation of accuracy by 71.13% for various non-IID degrees highlighting superior performance when faced with heterogeneous data. Ampere is available from https://github.com/blessonvar/Ampere."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 11 figures. Accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Parallel and Distributed Systems (TPDS)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:09Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:19:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Lupinacci","name":"Lupinacci, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco Aurelio","familyName":"Pironti","name":"Pironti, Francesco Aurelio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Blefari","name":"Blefari, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Romeo","name":"Romeo, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Arena","name":"Arena, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelo","familyName":"Furfaro","name":"Furfaro, Angelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Dark Side of LLMs: Agent-based Attack Vectors for System-level Compromise"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T13:54:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:41:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T15:18:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:41:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T09:50:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:26:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-06T15:27:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-08-07T00:49:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-04T10:28:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:37:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-09T07:47:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:39:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-35586-7_1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid adoption of Large Language Model (LLM) agents and multi-agent systems enables remarkable capabilities in natural language processing and generation. However, these systems introduce security vulnerabilities that extend beyond traditional content generation to system-level compromises. This paper presents a comprehensive evaluation of the LLMs security used as reasoning engines within autonomous agents, highlighting how they can be exploited as attack vectors capable of achieving computer takeovers. We focus on how different attack surfaces and trust boundaries can be leveraged to orchestrate such takeovers. We demonstrate that adversaries can effectively coerce popular LLMs into autonomously installing and executing malware on victim machines. Our evaluation of 18 state-of-the-art LLMs reveals that 94.4% of models succumb to Direct Prompt Injection, and 83.3% are vulnerable to the more stealthy and evasive RAG Backdoor Attack. Notably, we tested trust boundaries within multi-agent systems, where LLM agents interact and influence each other, and we revealed that LLMs which successfully resist direct injection or RAG backdoor attacks will execute identical payloads when requested by peer agents. We found that 100.0% of tested LLMs can be compromised through Inter-Agent Trust Exploitation attacks, and that every model exhibits context-dependent security behaviors that create exploitable blind spots."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-10T01:40:47Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:40:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:55:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoshuo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chaoshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Concept Unlearning by Modeling Key Steps of Diffusion Process"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T03:55:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:17:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T03:02:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:12:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T11:04:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:39:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T13:33:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:42:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tifs.2026.3723113","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image diffusion models remain susceptible to generating undesirable or harmful content. Although concept unlearning mitigates this risk, existing methods struggle with a critical optimization dilemma: thorough semantic erasure frequently induces the catastrophic forgetting of unrelated generative capabilities. To overcome this challenge, we propose Key Step Concept Unlearning (KSCU). Serving as an integrated methodological refinement deeply motivated by information theory, KSCU explores the profound impact of step scheduling order and reveals that traditional randomized timestep sampling severely disrupts trajectory dependency. We demonstrate that indiscriminately targeting the entire diffusion process is inefficient, as the optimal step range for unlearning inherently varies across different concepts. Rather than globally fine-tuning all timesteps, KSCU explicitly integrates a sequential-scheduling-based Key Step Table, CFG-aware leakage compensation, and prompt augmentation to dynamically isolate optimization to a concept-specific active region. This localized strategy successfully eradicates the target concept while preventing the structural collapse caused by early-step over-optimization. Consequently, KSCU significantly reduces computational overhead and establishes a state-of-the-art trade-off between concept erasure and utility retention. Comprehensive evaluations demonstrate that KSCU consistently delivers superior performance across diverse unlearning tasks, including nudity, style, object classes, and mass instance concepts. For example, in nudity removal, KSCU yields a 96.5% unlearning accuracy alongside a state-of-the-art FID of 14.1."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by T-IFS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-10T01:32:33Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:32:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:05:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08029","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08029","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08029","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ada","familyName":"Aka","name":"Aka, Ada","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Palikot","name":"Palikot, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nima","familyName":"Yazdani","name":"Yazdani, Nima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Better Together: Quantifying the Benefits of AI-Assisted Recruitment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T22:51:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T15:03:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:50:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hiring algorithms have mostly scored the materials recruiters already see. Large language models (LLMs) can instead generate new information about candidates by conducting, at scale, structured interviews once reserved for a few finalists. We study this shift in two field experiments at a recruitment platform. The first experiment holds the candidate pool fixed and randomizes whether recruiters observe the AI Interview Report; the second embeds the AI interview as a requirement in a live hiring pipeline. In both, candidates shortlisted with AI interview information pass the final human interview (conducted blind to shortlisting condition) at rates 17.5 (SE 8.5) to 20 (SE 11.8) percentage points higher than candidates shortlisted from resumes alone. The gains concentrate where resumes are least informative: adding AI Interview Report ratings to conventional candidate features raises out-of-sample AUC by 0.18 for junior candidates, against 0.08 for non-junior candidates. The participation cost falls on applicants as 75 percent of invited candidates do not complete the interview. However, the attrition is itself a signal: completion is more consistent with job-search motivation than with predicted interview performance. AI interviews thus add information exactly where conventional signals fail, and they move the cost of screening from firms to applicants."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08029","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-14T01:13:35Z","registered":"2025-07-14T01:13:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:05:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08027","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08027","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08027","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. Russell","familyName":"Neuman","name":"Neuman, W. Russell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chad","familyName":"Coleman","name":"Coleman, Chad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Dasdan","name":"Dasdan, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Safinah","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Safinah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manan","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Manan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kund","familyName":"Meghani","name":"Meghani, Kund","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"\"Amazing, They All Lean Left\" -- Analyzing the Political Temperaments of Current LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T21:19:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:50:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1080/29974100.2026.2667948","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies have revealed a consistent liberal orientation in the ethical and political responses generated by most commercial large language models (LLMs), yet the underlying causes and resulting implications remain unclear. This paper systematically investigates the political temperament of seven prominent LLMs - OpenAI's GPT-4o, Anthropic's Claude Sonnet 4, Perplexity (Sonar Large), Google's Gemini 2.5 Flash, Meta AI's Llama 4, Mistral 7b Le Chat and High-Flyer's DeepSeek R1 -- using a multi-pronged approach that includes Moral Foundations Theory, a dozen established political ideology scales and a new index of current political controversies. We find strong and consistent prioritization of liberal-leaning values, particularly care and fairness, across most models. Further analysis attributes this trend to four overlapping factors: Liberal-leaning training corpora, reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), the dominance of liberal frameworks in academic ethical discourse and safety-driven fine-tuning practices. We also distinguish between political \"bias\" and legitimate epistemic differences, cautioning against conflating the two. A comparison of base and fine-tuned model pairs reveals that fine-tuning generally increases liberal lean, an effect confirmed through both self-report and empirical testing. We argue that this \"liberal tilt\" is not a programming error or the personal preference of programmers but an emergent property of training on democratic rights-focused discourse. Finally, we propose that LLMs may indirectly echo John Rawls' famous veil-of ignorance philosophical aspiration, reflecting a moral stance unanchored to personal identity or interest. Rather than undermining democratic discourse, this pattern may offer a new lens through which to examine collective reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08027","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-14T01:13:32Z","registered":"2025-07-14T01:13:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:19:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emile","familyName":"Pierret","name":"Pierret, Emile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Galerne","name":"Galerne, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exact Evaluation of the Accuracy of Diffusion Models for Inverse Problems with Gaussian Data Distributions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T16:36:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-25T09:25:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:24:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Used as priors for Bayesian inverse problems, diffusion models have recently attracted considerable attention in the literature. Their flexibility and high variance enable them to generate multiple solutions for a given task, such as inpainting, super-resolution, and deblurring. However, there is still a lack of understanding about how accurately these conditional diffusion algorithms perform conditional sampling. In this article, we investigate the errors induced by these models when applied to a Gaussian data distribution for which the score function is exactly known. Within this constrained context, we are able to precisely analyze the discrepancy between the theoretical resolution of inverse problems via conditional sampling and the practical distributions generated by conditional diffusion models. This is done by characterizing all the involved iterative Gaussian processes and by computing the exact Wasserstein distance between the distributions of the diffusion model samplers and the ideal conditional distribution associated with the inverse problem. Our findings allow for the comparison of two major algorithms from the literature, Deep Posterior Sampling (DPS) and Pseudo-inverse Guided Diffusion Models ($Π$GDM), and the introduction of the new paradigm Conditional Gaussian Diffusion Models (CGDM) that is shown to be more accurate for Gaussian data distributions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:44:56Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:44:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:46:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Calum","familyName":"Brown","name":"Brown, Calum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Rounsevell","name":"Rounsevell, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Too Human to Model:The Uncanny Valley of LLMs in Social Simulation -- When Generative Language Agents Misalign with Modelling Principles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T18:02:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-21T01:30:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been increasingly used to build agents in social simulation because of their impressive abilities to generate fluent, contextually coherent dialogues. Such abilities can enhance the realism of models. However, the pursuit of realism is not necessarily compatible with the epistemic foundation of modelling. We argue that LLM agents, in many regards, are too human to model: they are too expressive, detailed and intractable to be consistent with the abstraction, simplification, and interpretability typically demanded by modelling. Through a model-building thought experiment that converts the Bass diffusion model to an LLM-based variant, we uncover five core dilemmas: a temporal resolution mismatch between natural conversation and abstract time steps; the need for intervention in conversations while avoiding undermining spontaneous agent outputs; the temptation to introduce rule-like instructions in prompts while maintaining conversational naturalness; the tension between role consistency and role evolution across time; and the challenge of understanding emergence, where system-level patterns become obscured by verbose micro textual outputs. These dilemmas steer the LLM agents towards an uncanny valley: not abstract enough to clarify underlying social mechanisms, while not natural enough to represent realistic human behaviour. This exposes an important paradox: the realism of LLM agents can obscure, rather than clarify, social dynamics when misapplied. We tease out the conditions in which LLM agents are ideally suited: where system-level emergence is not the focus, linguistic nuances and meaning are central, interactions unfold in natural time, and stable role identity is more important than long-term behavioural evolution. We call for repositioning LLM agents in the ecosystem of social simulation for future applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:27:09Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:27:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:41:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06722","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06722","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06722","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunwoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haneul","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Haneul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alice","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Alice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Effect of Uncertainty on Layer-wise Inference Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T10:30:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-26T07:52:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:29:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how large language models (LLMs) internally represent and process their predictions is central to detecting uncertainty and preventing hallucinations. While several studies have shown that models encode uncertainty in their hidden states, it is underexplored how this affects the way they process such hidden states. In this work, we demonstrate that the dynamics of output token probabilities across layers for certain and uncertain outputs are largely aligned, revealing that uncertainty does not seem to affect inference dynamics. Specifically, we use the Tuned Lens, a variant of the Logit Lens, to analyze the layer-wise probability trajectories of final prediction tokens across 11 datasets and 5 models. Using incorrect predictions as those with higher epistemic uncertainty, our results show aligned trajectories for certain and uncertain predictions that both observe abrupt increases in confidence at similar layers. We balance this finding by showing evidence that more competent models may learn to process uncertainty differently. Our findings challenge the feasibility of leveraging simplistic methods for detecting uncertainty at inference. More broadly, our work demonstrates how interpretability methods may be used to investigate the way uncertainty affects inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Actionable Interpretability Workshop - ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06722","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:37:29Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:37:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-29T01:58:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06419","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06419","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06419","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pankayaraj","familyName":"Pathmanathan","name":"Pathmanathan, Pankayaraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Furong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teach a Reward Model to Correct Itself: Reward Guided Adversarial Failure Discovery for Robust Reward Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T21:56:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-08T15:00:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-09T00:55:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-04T20:44:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:08:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reward modeling (RM), which captures human preferences to align large language models (LLMs), is increasingly employed in tasks such as model finetuning, response filtering, and ranking. However, due to the inherent complexity of human preferences and the limited coverage of available datasets, reward models often fail under distributional shifts or adversarial perturbations. Existing approaches for identifying such failure modes typically rely on prior knowledge about preference distributions or failure attributes, limiting their practicality in real-world settings where such information is unavailable. In this work, we propose a tractable, preference-distribution agnostic method for discovering reward model failure modes via reward guided controlled decoding. Building on this, we introduce REFORM, a self-improving reward modeling framework that enhances robustness by using the reward model itself to guide the generation of falsely scored responses. These adversarial examples are then used to augment the training data and patch the reward model's misaligned behavior. We evaluate REFORM on two widely used preference datasets Anthropic Helpful Harmless (HH) and PKU Beavertails and demonstrate that it significantly improves robustness without sacrificing reward quality. Notably, REFORM preserves performance both in direct evaluation and in downstream policy training, and further improves alignment quality by removing spurious correlations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06419","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:29:51Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:29:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:00:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Talor","familyName":"Abramovich","name":"Abramovich, Talor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gal","familyName":"Chechik","name":"Chechik, Gal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AblationBench: Evaluating Automated Planning of Ablations in Empirical AI Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T12:07:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T13:22:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:41:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T08:32:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:49:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language model agents are increasingly used to automate scientific research, yet evaluating their scientific contributions remains a challenge. A key mechanism to obtain such insights is through ablation experiments. To this end, we introduce AblationBench, a benchmark suite for evaluating agents on ablation planning tasks in empirical AI research. It includes two tasks: AuthorAblation, which helps authors propose ablation experiments based on a method section and contains 83 instances, and ReviewerAblation, which helps reviewers find missing ablations in a full paper and contains 350 instances. For both tasks, we develop LM-based judges that serve as an automatic evaluation framework. Our experiments with frontier LMs show that these tasks remain challenging, with the best-performing LM system identifying only 45% of the original ablations on average, below human-level performance. We observe an inverse performance trend between the author and reviewer tasks, which we attribute to differences in model grounding. Lastly, we analyze the limitations of current LMs on these tasks, and find that chain-of-thought prompting outperforms an agent-based approach. Our data is available on https://huggingface.co/collections/ai-coscientist/ablationbench, and our code is available on https://github.com/ai-scientist-bench/ablation-bench ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"AI4Science Workshop, ICML 2026; Project page: https://ablation-bench.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-14T01:13:48Z","registered":"2025-07-14T01:13:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:50:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yahan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masafumi","familyName":"Oyamada","name":"Oyamada, Masafumi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Deliberately, Acting Intuitively: Unlocking Test-Time Reasoning in Multimodal LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T16:25:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:49:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T11:19:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:57:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning is essential for large language models (LLMs), especially in complex tasks such as mathematical problem solving. However, multimodal reasoning still faces challenges in modality alignment and training scalability, as many existing methods rely on additional annotations or complex rule-based rewards. To address these issues, we propose the Deliberate-to-Intuitive reasoning framework (D2I), which improves the understanding and reasoning abilities of multimodal LLMs (MLLMs) without extra annotations or complex rewards. During training, D2I uses deliberate reasoning strategies supervised only by rule-based format rewards to enhance modality alignment. During inference, it shifts to intuitive reasoning by removing these explicit strategies, allowing the model to implicitly apply the acquired abilities in its responses. D2I outperforms baselines on both in-domain and out-of-domain benchmarks, highlighting the effectiveness of format rewards in fostering transferable multimodal reasoning skills and suggesting the benefit of decoupling training-time reasoning depth from test-time response flexibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 24 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:44:43Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:44:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:52:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Bochkov","name":"Bochkov, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Growing Transformers: Modular Composition and Layer-wise Expansion on a Frozen Substrate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T20:01:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:00:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T18:17:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T02:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-02T22:37:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:45:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study a constrained training regime for decoder-only Transformers in which the token interface is fixed, previously trained dense blocks are not reopened, and the active trainable parameter set is kept approximately constant as depth grows. Starting from a shallow model, we stack new blocks and train only the newest blocks and the LM head; optional LoRA phases provide limited global readjustment under the same active-parameter budget. The paper asks a feasibility/tradeoff question, not whether this regime matches tuned monolithic pretraining.\n In a common-protocol 9-layer study on a frozen Unicode substrate, the constructive frozen-Unicode model uses 105.0M active trainable parameters, compared with 180.5M for the interface-matched monolithic frozen baseline and 247.6M for the fully trainable monolithic baseline. We then consider an extreme fixed interface: each token is represented only by a frozen 16-dim binary token-ID code, deterministically lifted to d_model, so the resulting token embedding matrix has rank at most 16. Even in this setting, continued growth remains viable. In a 68.9B-token run on FineWeb-Edu + Cosmopedia, a 16-layer 269.7M model trained above this fixed interface reaches 28.92\\% MMLU after an interleaved LoRA stage. Reported final metrics are measured after merging the last-stage LoRA adapters into the 269.7M base model. Because the data mixture changes across stages in this long-horizon run, we interpret it as a viability demonstration rather than a clean causal comparison.\n Overall, the evidence supports a narrow claim: useful continued learning can proceed above a frozen minimal interface under a bounded active trainable-parameter budget, with a clear tradeoff against dense monolithic training in final perplexity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Limitations added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:07Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T03:58:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhailong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenhailong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuehang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xuehang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofia","familyName":"Stoica","name":"Stoica, Sofia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongru","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeonjeong","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Hyeonjeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiusi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiusi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Perception-Aware Policy Optimization for Multimodal Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T23:22:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:09:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-13T16:48:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-15T00:48:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-31T17:54:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-01T00:52:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-07T04:33:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-08-08T00:18:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-14T16:31:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-15T01:06:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has proven to be a highly effective strategy for endowing Large Language Models (LLMs) with robust multi-step reasoning abilities. However, its design and optimizations remain tailored to purely textual domains, resulting in suboptimal performance when applied to multimodal reasoning tasks. In particular, we observe that a major source of error in current multimodal reasoning lies in the perception of visual inputs. To address this bottleneck, we propose PAPO, a novel policy gradient algorithm that encourages the model to learn to perceive while learning to reason. Specifically, we introduce the Implicit Perception Loss in the form of a KL divergence term, which can be seamlessly plugged into mainstream RLVR algorithms such as GRPO and DAPO. Notably, PAPO does not rely on additional data curation, reward models, or stronger teacher models. To further enhance the training stability of PAPO, we introduce the Double Entropy Loss, which effectively regularizes the new KL objective without compromising performance. Despite its simplicity, PAPO yields significant overall improvements of 4.4%-17.5% on diverse multimodal benchmarks. The improvements are more pronounced, approaching 8.0%-19.1%, on tasks with high vision dependency. We also observe a substantial reduction of 30.5% in perception errors, indicating improved perceptual capabilities with PAPO. Overall, our work introduces a deeper integration of perception-aware supervision into core learning objectives and lays the groundwork for a new RL framework that encourages visually grounded reasoning. Code and data will be made publicly available for research purposes. Project page: https://mikewangwzhl.github.io/PAPO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:30:34Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:30:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-15T02:22:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghyun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Yonghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chieh-Hsin","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Chieh-Hsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Hayakawa","name":"Hayakawa, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhta","familyName":"Takida","name":"Takida, Yuhta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoki","familyName":"Murata","name":"Murata, Naoki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Hsiang","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Wei-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woosung","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Woosung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kin Wai","familyName":"Cheuk","name":"Cheuk, Kin Wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junghyun","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Junghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Concept-TRAK: Understanding how diffusion models learn concepts through concept-level attribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T05:03:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:20:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-25T06:06:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-28T00:18:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T18:40:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:29:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While diffusion models excel at image generation, their growing adoption raises critical concerns about copyright issues and model transparency. Existing attribution methods identify training examples influencing an entire image, but fall short in isolating contributions to specific elements, such as styles or objects, that are of primary concern to stakeholders. To address this gap, we introduce concept-level attribution through a novel method called Concept-TRAK, which extends influence functions with a key innovation: specialized training and utility loss functions designed to isolate concept-specific influences rather than overall reconstruction quality. We evaluate Concept-TRAK on novel concept attribution benchmarks using Synthetic and CelebA-HQ datasets, as well as the established AbC benchmark, showing substantial improvements over prior methods in concept-level attribution scenarios. We further demonstrate its versatility on real-world text-to-image generation with compositional and multi-concept prompts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:33:06Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:33:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:39:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07139","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07139","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07139","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Renyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"See-Kiong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, See-Kiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Image Can Bring Your Memory Back: A Novel Multi-Modal Guided Attack against Image Generation Model Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T02:59:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:00:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:42:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image generation models (IGMs), particularly diffusion-based architectures such as Stable Diffusion (SD), have markedly enhanced the quality and diversity of AI-generated visual content. However, their generative capability has also raised significant ethical, legal, and societal concerns, including the potential to produce harmful, misleading, or copyright-infringing content. To mitigate these concerns, machine unlearning (MU) emerges as a promising solution by selectively removing undesirable concepts from pretrained models. Nevertheless, the robustness and effectiveness of existing unlearning techniques remain largely unexplored, particularly in the presence of multi-modal adversarial inputs.\n To bridge this gap, we propose Recall, a novel adversarial framework explicitly designed to compromise the robustness of unlearned IGMs. Unlike existing approaches that predominantly rely on adversarial text prompts, Recall exploits the intrinsic multi-modal conditioning capabilities of diffusion models by efficiently optimizing adversarial image prompts with guidance from a single semantically relevant reference image. Extensive experiments across ten state-of-the-art unlearning methods and diverse tasks show that Recall consistently outperforms existing baselines in terms of adversarial effectiveness, computational efficiency, and semantic fidelity with the original textual prompt. These findings reveal critical vulnerabilities in current unlearning mechanisms and underscore the need for more robust solutions to ensure the safety and reliability of generative models. Code and data are publicly available at \\textcolor{blue}{https://github.com/ryliu68/RECALL}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07139","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:21Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:42:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyon","familyName":"Vafa","name":"Vafa, Keyon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter G.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Peter G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashesh","familyName":"Rambachan","name":"Rambachan, Ashesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sendhil","familyName":"Mullainathan","name":"Mullainathan, Sendhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Has a Foundation Model Found? Using Inductive Bias to Probe for World Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T15:36:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:46:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T16:01:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:43:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-14T18:55:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-18T00:04:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-27T15:25:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-30T01:22:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models are premised on the idea that sequence prediction can uncover deeper domain understanding, much like how Kepler's predictions of planetary motion later led to the discovery of Newtonian mechanics. However, evaluating whether these models truly capture deeper structure remains a challenge. We develop a technique for evaluating foundation models that examines how they adapt to synthetic datasets generated from some postulated world model. Our technique measures whether the foundation model's inductive bias aligns with the world model, and so we refer to it as an inductive bias probe. Across multiple domains, we find that foundation models can excel at their training tasks yet fail to develop inductive biases towards the underlying world model when adapted to new tasks. We particularly find that foundation models trained on orbital trajectories consistently fail to apply Newtonian mechanics when adapted to new physics tasks. Further analysis reveals that these models behave as if they develop task-specific heuristics that fail to generalize."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:43:31Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:43:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-30T03:48:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06489","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06489","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06489","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen","familyName":"Obadinma","name":"Obadinma, Stephen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiaodan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T02:19:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:13:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T20:43:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:06:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-18T02:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:19:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robust verbal confidence generated by large language models (LLMs) is crucial for the deployment of LLMs to help ensure transparency, trust, and safety in many applications, including those involving human-AI interactions. In this paper, we present the first comprehensive study on the robustness of verbal confidence under adversarial attacks. We introduce attack frameworks targeting verbal confidence scores through both perturbation and jailbreak-based methods, and demonstrate that these attacks can significantly impair verbal confidence estimates and lead to frequent answer changes. We examine a variety of prompting strategies, model sizes, and application domains, revealing that current verbal confidence is vulnerable and that commonly used defence techniques are largely ineffective or counterproductive. Our findings underscore the need to design robust mechanisms for confidence expression in LLMs, as even subtle semantic-preserving modifications can lead to misleading confidence in responses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06489","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:31:36Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:31:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-19T03:15:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Marek","name":"Marek, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanae","familyName":"Lotfi","name":"Lotfi, Sanae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Somasundaram","name":"Somasundaram, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew Gordon","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Andrew Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Micah","familyName":"Goldblum","name":"Goldblum, Micah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Batch Size Training for Language Models: When Vanilla SGD Works, and Why Gradient Accumulation Is Wasteful"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T17:57:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:53:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-27T16:54:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-28T00:48:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T03:05:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:19:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-15T22:25:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:09:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional wisdom dictates that small batch sizes make language model pretraining and fine-tuning unstable, motivating gradient accumulation, which trades off the number of optimizer steps for a proportional increase in batch size. While it is common to decrease the learning rate for smaller batch sizes, other hyperparameters are often held fixed. In this work, we revisit small batch sizes all the way down to batch size one, and we propose a rule for scaling Adam hyperparameters to small batch sizes. In particular, rather than holding the decay rate of the second moment fixed across batch sizes, we propose to hold its half-life fixed in terms of tokens. We find that small batch sizes (1) train stably, (2) are consistently more robust to hyperparameter choices, (3) achieve equal or better per-FLOP performance than larger batch sizes, and (4) notably enable stable language model training with vanilla SGD, even without momentum, despite storing no optimizer state. Building on these results, we provide practical recommendations for selecting a batch size and setting optimizer hyperparameters. We further recommend against gradient accumulation unless training on multiple devices with multiple model replicas. Finally, we show that a small batch size combined with an optimizer with a small state size can provide the performance benefits of full fine-tuning while maintaining a similar memory footprint to LoRA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. Code available at: https://github.com/martin-marek/batch-size"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:47:18Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:47:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:07:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liliang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Liliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congcong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Congcong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young Jin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Young Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Atkinson","name":"Atkinson, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiankai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Baolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuohang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yelong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoder-Hybrid-Decoder Architecture for Efficient Reasoning with Long Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:27:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:25:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-16T07:00:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-17T00:23:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-25T01:16:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:20:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in language modeling have demonstrated the effectiveness of State Space Models (SSMs) for efficient sequence modeling. While hybrid architectures such as Samba and the decoder-decoder architecture, YOCO, have shown promising performance gains over Transformers, prior works have not investigated the efficiency potential of representation sharing between SSM layers. In this paper, we introduce the Gated Memory Unit (GMU), a simple yet effective mechanism for efficient memory sharing across layers. We apply it to create SambaY, a decoder-hybrid-decoder architecture that incorporates GMUs in the cross-decoder to share memory readout states from a Samba-based self-decoder. SambaY significantly enhances decoding efficiency, preserves linear pre-filling time complexity, and boosts long-context performance, all while eliminating the need for explicit positional encoding. Through extensive scaling experiments, we demonstrate that our model exhibits a significantly lower irreducible loss compared to a strong YOCO baseline, indicating superior performance scalability under large-scale compute regimes. Our largest model enhanced with Differential Attention, Phi4-mini-Flash-Reasoning, achieves significantly better performance than Phi4-mini-Reasoning on reasoning tasks such as Math500, AIME24/25, and GPQA Diamond without any reinforcement learning, while delivering up to 10x higher decoding throughput on 2K-length prompts with 32K generation length under the vLLM inference framework. We release our training codebase on open-source data at https://github.com/microsoft/ArchScale."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025. Camera-ready Version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:34:35Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:34:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:59:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06485","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06485","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06485","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehong","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Jaehong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoubin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Shoubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Mohaiminul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Md Mohaiminul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gedas","familyName":"Bertasius","name":"Bertasius, Gedas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-RTS: Rethinking Reinforcement Learning and Test-Time Scaling for Efficient and Enhanced Video Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T02:06:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:13:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T16:19:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite advances in reinforcement learning (RL)-based video reasoning with large language models (LLMs), data collection and fine-tuning remain significant challenges. These methods often rely on large-scale supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with extensive video data and long Chain-of-Thought (CoT) annotations, making them costly and hard to scale. To address this, we present Video-RTS, a new approach to improve video reasoning capability with drastically improved data efficiency by combining data-efficient RL with a video-adaptive test-time scaling (TTS) strategy. Building on observations about the data scaling, we skip the resource-intensive SFT step and employ efficient pure-RL training with output-based rewards, requiring no additional annotations or extensive fine-tuning. Furthermore, to utilize computational resources more efficiently, we introduce a sparse-to-dense video TTS strategy that improves inference by iteratively adding frames based on output consistency. We validate our approach on multiple video reasoning benchmarks, showing that Video-RTS surpasses existing video reasoning models by 2.4% in accuracy using only 3.6% training samples. Specifically, Video-RTS achieves a 4.2% improvement on Video-Holmes, a recent and challenging video reasoning benchmark. Notably, our pure RL training and adaptive video TTS offer complementary strengths, enabling Video-RTS's strong reasoning performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025. The first two authors contributed equally. Project page: https://sites.google.com/cs.unc.edu/videorts2025/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06485","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:31:30Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:31:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:09:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06795","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06795","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06795","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seonwu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seonwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Na","name":"Na, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kihun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kihun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanhee","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Hanhee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geun","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Geun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mintae","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Mintae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seongik","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Seongik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ki Hyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Ki Hyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngsub","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Youngsub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byoung-Ki","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Byoung-Ki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ixi-GEN: Efficient Industrial sLLMs through Domain Adaptive Continual Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T12:30:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:38:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T07:05:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:19:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T14:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:55:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-23T06:41:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:30:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of open-source large language models (LLMs) has expanded opportunities for enterprise applications; however, many organizations still lack the infrastructure to deploy and maintain large-scale models. As a result, small LLMs (sLLMs) have become a practical alternative despite inherent performance limitations. While Domain Adaptive Continual Pretraining (DACP) has been explored for domain adaptation, its utility in commercial settings remains under-examined. In this study, we validate the effectiveness of a DACP-based recipe across diverse foundation models and service domains, producing DACP-applied sLLMs (ixi-GEN). Through extensive experiments and real-world evaluations, we demonstrate that ixi-GEN models achieve substantial gains in target-domain performance while preserving general capabilities, offering a cost-efficient and scalable solution for enterprise-level deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025 Industry Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06795","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:39:18Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:39:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-24T02:13:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kecheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Lihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiling","familyName":"Zhen","name":"Zhen, Huiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxuan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zili","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Zili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Attention-Aware GNN-based Input Defense against Multi-Turn LLM Jailbreak"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:55:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T04:56:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T04:28:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:22:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have gained significant traction in various applications, yet their capabilities present risks for both constructive and malicious exploitation. Despite extensive training and fine-tuning efforts aimed at enhancing safety, LLMs remain susceptible to jailbreak attacks. Recently, the emergence of multi-turn attacks has intensified this vulnerability. Unlike single-turn attacks, multi-turn attacks incrementally escalate dialogue complexity, rendering them more challenging to detect and mitigate.\n In this study, we introduce G-Guard, an innovative attention-aware Graph Neural Network (GNN)-based input classifier specifically designed to defend against multi-turn jailbreak attacks targeting LLMs. G-Guard constructs an entity graph for multi-turn queries, which captures the interrelationships between queries and harmful keywords that present in multi-turn queries. Furthermore, we propose an attention-aware augmentation mechanism that retrieves the most relevant single-turn query based on the ongoing multi-turn conversation. The retrieved query is incorporated as a labeled node within the graph, thereby enhancing the GNN's capacity to classify the current query as harmful or benign. Evaluation results show that G-Guard consistently outperforms all baselines across diverse datasets and evaluation metrics, demonstrating its efficacy as a robust defense mechanism against multi-turn jailbreak attacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:31Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:19:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08870","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08870","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08870","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaowenqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yaowenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Bingxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"You","name":"You, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUIDE: Towards Scalable Advising for Research Ideas"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T17:59:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-15T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-05T00:39:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of AI research is advancing at an unprecedented pace, enabling automated hypothesis generation and experimental design across diverse domains such as biology, mathematics, and artificial intelligence. Despite these advancements, there remains a significant gap in the availability of scalable advising systems capable of providing high-quality, well-reasoned feedback to refine proposed hypotheses and experimental designs. To address this challenge, we explore key factors that underlie the development of robust advising systems, including model size, context length, confidence estimation, and structured reasoning processes. Our findings reveal that a relatively small model, when equipped with a well-compressed literature database and a structured reasoning framework, can outperform powerful general-purpose language models such as Deepseek-R1 in terms of acceptance rates for self-ranked top-30% submissions to ICLR 2025. Moreover, when limited to high-confidence predictions, our system achieves an acceptance rate exceeding 90% on the ICLR 2025 test set, underscoring its potential to significantly enhance the quality and efficiency of hypothesis generation and experimental design. The code is released at https://github.com/HowardLiu0830/GUIDE-Research-Idea-Evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08870","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-15T02:17:49Z","registered":"2025-07-15T02:17:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:47:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06554","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06554","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06554","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zou","familyName":"Yuheng","name":"Yuheng, Zou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Yiran","name":"Yiran, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Yuzhu","name":"Yuzhu, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huang","familyName":"Yanhua","name":"Yanhua, Huang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEARA: An Automated Approach for Obtaining Optimal Retrievers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T05:13:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:20:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-28T02:55:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:52:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a core approach for enhancing Large Language Models (LLMs), where the effectiveness of the retriever largely determines the overall response quality of RAG systems. Retrievers encompass a multitude of hyperparameters that significantly impact performance outcomes and demonstrate sensitivity to specific applications. Nevertheless, hyperparameter optimization entails prohibitively high computational expenses. Existing evaluation methods suffer from either prohibitive costs or disconnection from domain-specific scenarios. This paper proposes SEARA (Subset sampling Evaluation for Automatic Retriever Assessment), which addresses evaluation data challenges through subset sampling techniques and achieves robust automated retriever evaluation by minimal retrieval facts extraction and comprehensive retrieval metrics. Based on real user queries, this method enables fully automated retriever evaluation at low cost, thereby obtaining optimal retriever for specific business scenarios. We validate our method across classic RAG applications in rednote, including knowledge-based Q\\\u0026amp;A system and retrieval-based travel assistant, successfully obtaining scenario-specific optimal retrievers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06554","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:33:16Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:33:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:14:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06899","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06899","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06899","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"You","name":"You, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuli","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Fuli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tat-Seng","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Tat-Seng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VisualTrap: A Stealthy Backdoor Attack on GUI Agents via Visual Grounding Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T14:36:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:43:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T14:33:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:53:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical User Interface (GUI) agents powered by Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have emerged as a revolutionary approach to automating human-machine interactions, capable of autonomously operating personal devices (e.g., mobile phones) or applications within the device to perform complex real-world tasks in a human-like manner. However, their close integration with personal devices raises significant security concerns, with many threats, including backdoor attacks, remaining largely unexplored. This work reveals that the visual grounding of GUI agent-mapping textual plans to GUI elements-can introduce vulnerabilities, enabling new types of backdoor attacks. With backdoor attack targeting visual grounding, the agent's behavior can be compromised even when given correct task-solving plans. To validate this vulnerability, we propose VisualTrap, a method that can hijack the grounding by misleading the agent to locate textual plans to trigger locations instead of the intended targets. VisualTrap uses the common method of injecting poisoned data for attacks, and does so during the pre-training of visual grounding to ensure practical feasibility of attacking. Empirical results show that VisualTrap can effectively hijack visual grounding with as little as 5% poisoned data and highly stealthy visual triggers (invisible to the human eye); and the attack can be generalized to downstream tasks, even after clean fine-tuning. Moreover, the injected trigger can remain effective across different GUI environments, e.g., being trained on mobile/web and generalizing to desktop environments. These findings underscore the urgent need for further research on backdoor attack risks in GUI agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in COLM2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06899","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:42:10Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:42:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T02:28:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08851","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08851","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08851","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Schwaiger","name":"Schwaiger, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Thalhammer","name":"Thalhammer, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wilfried","familyName":"Wöber","name":"Wöber, Wilfried","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerald","familyName":"Steinbauer-Wagner","name":"Steinbauer-Wagner, Gerald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OTAS: Open-vocabulary Token Alignment for Outdoor Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T22:49:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-15T00:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T12:26:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:28:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding open-world semantics is critical for robotic planning and control, particularly in unstructured outdoor environments. Existing vision-language mapping approaches typically rely on object-centric segmentation priors, which often fail outdoors due to semantic ambiguities and indistinct class boundaries. We propose OTAS - an Open-vocabulary Token Alignment method for outdoor Segmentation. OTAS addresses the limitations of open-vocabulary segmentation models by extracting semantic structure directly from the output tokens of pre-trained vision models. By clustering semantically similar structures across single and multiple views and grounding them in language, OTAS reconstructs a geometrically consistent feature field that supports open-vocabulary segmentation queries. Our method operates in a zero-shot manner, without scene-specific fine-tuning, and achieves real-time performance of up to ~17 fps. On the Off-Road Freespace Detection dataset, OTAS yields a modest IoU improvement over fine-tuned and open-vocabulary 2D segmentation baselines. In 3D segmentation on TartanAir, it achieves up to a 151% relative IoU improvement compared to existing open-vocabulary mapping methods. Real-world reconstructions further demonstrate OTAS' applicability to robotic deployment. Code and a ROS 2 node are available at https://otas-segmentation.github.io/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08851","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-15T02:17:22Z","registered":"2025-07-15T02:17:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:54:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingqin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mingqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linchao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Linchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji-Zhe","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ji-Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancheng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Jiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restoration via Gradient Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T08:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:28:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T05:42:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-04T00:23:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746027.3754582","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have shown remarkable promise for image restoration by leveraging powerful priors. Prominent methods typically frame the restoration problem within a Bayesian inference framework, which iteratively combines a denoising step with a likelihood guidance step. However, the interactions between these two components in the generation process remain underexplored. In this paper, we analyze the underlying gradient dynamics of these components and identify significant instabilities. Specifically, we demonstrate conflicts between the prior and likelihood gradient directions, alongside temporal fluctuations in the likelihood gradient itself. We show that these instabilities disrupt the generative process and compromise restoration performance. To address these issues, we propose Stabilized Progressive Gradient Diffusion (SPGD), a novel gradient management technique. SPGD integrates two synergistic components: (1) a progressive likelihood warm-up strategy to mitigate gradient conflicts; and (2) adaptive directional momentum (ADM) smoothing to reduce fluctuations in the likelihood gradient. Extensive experiments across diverse restoration tasks demonstrate that SPGD significantly enhances generation stability, leading to state-of-the-art performance in quantitative metrics and visually superior results. Code is available at https://github.com/74587887/SPGD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACM Multimedia 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-10T01:35:49Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:35:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-04T02:03:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshita","familyName":"Bhagia","name":"Bhagia, Akshita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Farhat","name":"Farhat, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Muennighoff","name":"Muennighoff, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pete","familyName":"Walsh","name":"Walsh, Pete","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Morrison","name":"Morrison, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dustin","familyName":"Schwenk","name":"Schwenk, Dustin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shayne","familyName":"Longpre","name":"Longpre, Shayne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jake","familyName":"Poznanski","name":"Poznanski, Jake","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Allyson","familyName":"Ettinger","name":"Ettinger, Allyson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daogao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daogao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Margaret","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Margaret","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dirk","familyName":"Groeneveld","name":"Groeneveld, Dirk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-tau","familyName":"Yih","name":"Yih, Wen-tau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Soldaini","name":"Soldaini, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah A.","familyName":"Smith","name":"Smith, Noah A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Zettlemoyer","name":"Zettlemoyer, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pang Wei","familyName":"Koh","name":"Koh, Pang Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannaneh","familyName":"Hajishirzi","name":"Hajishirzi, Hannaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Farhadi","name":"Farhadi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sewon","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Sewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlexOlmo: Open Language Models for Flexible Data Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T16:54:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:50:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T23:12:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-15T00:09:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-02T21:10:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-05T00:36:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-23T00:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-08-26T00:11:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce FlexOlmo, a new class of language models (LMs) that supports (1) distributed training without data sharing, where different model parameters are independently trained on closed datasets, and (2) data-flexible inference, where these parameters along with their associated data can be flexibly included or excluded from model inferences with no further training. FlexOlmo employs a mixture-of-experts (MoE) architecture where each expert is trained independently on closed datasets and later integrated through a new domain-informed routing without any joint training. FlexOlmo is trained on FlexMix, a corpus we curate comprising publicly available datasets alongside seven domain-specific sets, representing realistic approximations of closed sets. We evaluate models with up to 37 billion parameters (20 billion active) on 31 diverse downstream tasks. We show that a general expert trained on public data can be effectively combined with independently trained experts from other data owners, leading to an average 41% relative improvement while allowing users to opt out of certain data based on data licensing or permission requirements. Our approach also outperforms prior model merging methods by 10.1% on average and surpasses the standard MoE trained without data restrictions using the same training FLOPs. Altogether, this research presents a solution for both data owners and researchers in regulated industries with sensitive or protected data. FlexOlmo enables benefiting from closed data while respecting data owners' preferences by keeping their data local and supporting fine-grained control of data access during inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:45:22Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:45:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-26T03:19:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan-Yun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Fan-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shengguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Yim","name":"Yim, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Zook","name":"Zook, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangru","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shangru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Hsin","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Yu-Hsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethem","familyName":"Can","name":"Can, Ethem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunlei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clemens","familyName":"Eppner","name":"Eppner, Clemens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valts","familyName":"Blukis","name":"Blukis, Valts","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Tremblay","name":"Tremblay, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stan","familyName":"Birchfield","name":"Birchfield, Stan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Haber","name":"Haber, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"3D-Generalist: Self-Improving Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T02:00:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:12:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-19T19:36:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-21T00:06:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite large-scale pretraining endowing models with language and vision reasoning capabilities, improving their spatial reasoning capability remains challenging due to the lack of data grounded in the 3D world. While it is possible for humans to manually create immersive and interactive worlds through 3D graphics, as seen in applications such as VR, gaming, and robotics, this process remains highly labor-intensive. In this paper, we propose a scalable method for generating high-quality 3D environments that can serve as training data for foundation models. We recast 3D environment building as a sequential decision-making problem, employing Vision-Language-Models (VLMs) as policies that output actions to jointly craft a 3D environment's layout, materials, lighting, and assets. Our proposed framework, 3D-Generalist, trains VLMs to generate more prompt-aligned 3D environments via self-improvement fine-tuning. We demonstrate the effectiveness of 3D-Generalist and the proposed training strategy in generating simulation-ready 3D environments. Furthermore, we demonstrate its quality and scalability in synthetic data generation by pretraining a vision foundation model on the generated data. After fine-tuning the pre-trained model on downstream tasks, we show that it surpasses models pre-trained on meticulously human-crafted synthetic data and approaches results achieved with real data orders of magnitude larger."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"project website: https://ai.stanford.edu/~sunfanyun/3d-generalist/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:31:29Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:31:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-21T01:53:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06306","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06306","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06306","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"Rathi","name":"Rathi, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Jurafsky","name":"Jurafsky, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitlyn","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kaitlyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Humans overrely on overconfident language models, across languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T18:01:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-08T00:50:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-11T00:13:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are deployed globally, it is crucial that their responses are calibrated across languages to accurately convey uncertainty and limitations. Prior work shows that LLMs are linguistically overconfident in English, leading users to overrely on confident generations. However, the usage and interpretation of epistemic markers (e.g., 'I think it's') differs sharply across languages. Here, we study the risks of multilingual linguistic (mis)calibration, overconfidence, and overreliance across five languages to evaluate LLM safety in a global context. Our work finds that overreliance risks are high across languages. We first analyze the distribution of LLM-generated epistemic markers and observe that LLMs are overconfident across languages, frequently generating strengtheners even as part of incorrect responses. Model generations are, however, sensitive to documented cross-linguistic variation in usage: for example, models generate the most markers of uncertainty in Japanese and the most markers of certainty in German and Mandarin. Next, we measure human reliance rates across languages, finding that reliance behaviors differ cross-linguistically: for example, participants are significantly more likely to discount expressions of uncertainty in Japanese than in English (i.e., ignore their 'hedging' function and rely on generations that contain them). Taken together, these results indicate a high risk of reliance on overconfident model generations across languages. Our findings highlight the challenges of multilingual linguistic calibration and stress the importance of culturally and linguistically contextualized model safety evaluations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"camera ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06306","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:27:04Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:27:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-11T01:57:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Shaowu","name":"Shaowu, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Xibin","name":"Xibin, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gao","familyName":"Junyu","name":"Junyu, Gao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sun","familyName":"Qianmei","name":"Qianmei, Sun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Chao","name":"Chao, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:22:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-01T08:19:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-04T00:27:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-term action recognition (LTAR) is challenging due to extended temporal spans with complex atomic action correlations and visual confounders. Although vision-language models (VLMs) have shown promise, they often rely on statistical correlations instead of causal mechanisms. Moreover, existing causality-based methods address modal-specific biases but lack cross-modal causal modeling, limiting their utility in VLM-based LTAR. This paper proposes \\textbf{C}ross-\\textbf{M}odal \\textbf{D}ual-\\textbf{C}ausal \\textbf{L}earning (CMDCL), which introduces a structural causal model to uncover causal relationships between videos and label texts.\n CMDCL addresses cross-modal biases in text embeddings via textual causal intervention and removes confounders inherent in the visual modality through visual causal intervention guided by the debiased text.\n These dual-causal interventions enable robust action representations to address LTAR challenges. Experimental results on three benchmarks including Charades, Breakfast and COIN, demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed model. Our code is available at https://github.com/xushaowu/CMDCL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:34:30Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:34:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-04T01:48:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.21107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.21107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.21107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"Manson","name":"Manson, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curved Inference: Concern-Sensitive Geometry in Large Language Model Residual Streams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T23:05:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-30T00:01:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose Curved Inference - a geometric Interpretability framework that tracks how the residual stream trajectory of a large language model bends in response to shifts in semantic concern. Across 20 matched prompts spanning emotional, moral, perspective, logical, identity, environmental, and nonsense domains, we analyse Gemma3-1b and LLaMA3.2-3b using five native-space metrics, with a primary focus on curvature (\\k{appa}_i) and salience (S(t)). These metrics are computed under a pullback semantic metric derived from the unembedding matrix, ensuring that all measurements reflect token-aligned geometry rather than raw coordinate structure. We find that concern-shifted prompts reliably alter internal activation trajectories in both models - with LLaMA exhibiting consistent, statistically significant scaling in both curvature and salience as concern intensity increases. Gemma also responds to concern but shows weaker differentiation between moderate and strong variants. Our results support a two-layer view of LLM geometry - a latent conceptual structure encoded in the embedding space, and a contextual trajectory shaped by prompt-specific inference. Curved Inference reveals how models navigate, reorient, or reinforce semantic meaning over depth, offering a principled method for diagnosing alignment, abstraction, and emergent inference dynamics. These findings offer fresh insight into semantic abstraction and model alignment through the lens of Curved Inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 22 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.21107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-30T01:26:55Z","registered":"2025-07-30T01:26:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-30T01:26:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Ghiasvand","familyName":"Mohammadkhani","name":"Mohammadkhani, Mohammad Ghiasvand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Beigy","name":"Beigy, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Checklist Engineering Empowers Multilingual LLM Judges"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T12:03:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:36:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-27T08:42:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-29T00:38:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated text evaluation has long been a central issue in Natural Language Processing (NLP). Recently, the field has shifted toward using Large Language Models (LLMs) as evaluators-a trend known as the LLM-as-a-Judge paradigm. While promising and easily adaptable across tasks, this approach has seen limited exploration in multilingual contexts. Existing multilingual studies often rely on proprietary models or require extensive training data for fine-tuning, raising concerns about cost, time, and efficiency. In this paper, we propose Checklist Engineering based LLM-as-a-Judge (CE-Judge), a training-free framework that uses checklist intuition for multilingual evaluation with an open-source model. Experiments across multiple languages and three benchmark datasets, under both pointwise and pairwise settings, show that our method generally surpasses the baselines and performs on par with the GPT-4o model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:38:47Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:38:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-29T02:45:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06565","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06565","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06565","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan B.","familyName":"Gutiérrez","name":"Gutiérrez, Juan B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Mathematical Theory of Discursive Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.11; G.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T01, 60J10, 91D30, 05C82, 68T50, 68W20, 94A15","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T05:39:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:21:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T19:34:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-15T17:19:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-16T01:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-21T14:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07-22T01:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07-23T17:02:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-07-24T00:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) turn writing into a live exchange between humans and software. We characterize this new medium as a discursive network that treats people and LLMs as equal nodes and tracks how their statements circulate. We define the generation of erroneous information as invalidation (any factual, logical, or structural breach) and show it follows four hazards: drift from truth, self-repair, fresh fabrication, and external detection. We develop a general mathematical model of discursive networks that shows that a network governed only by drift and self-repair stabilizes at a modest error rate. Giving each false claim even a small chance of peer review shifts the system to a truth-dominant state. We operationalize peer review with the open-source Flaws-of-Others (FOO) algorithm: a configurable loop in which any set of agents critique one another while a harmonizer merges their verdicts. We identify an ethical transgression, epithesis, that occurs when humans fail to engage in the discursive network. The takeaway is practical and cultural: reliability in this new medium comes not from perfecting single models but from connecting imperfect ones into networks that enforce mutual accountability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 4 figures, 4 tables, 3 algorithm, 61 references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06565","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:33:32Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:33:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-24T02:00:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06482","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06482","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06482","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedDifRC: Unlocking the Potential of Text-to-Image Diffusion Models in Heterogeneous Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T01:57:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:12:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-18T06:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-21T00:19:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated learning aims at training models collaboratively across participants while protecting privacy. However, one major challenge for this paradigm is the data heterogeneity issue, where biased data preferences across multiple clients, harming the model's convergence and performance. In this paper, we first introduce powerful diffusion models into the federated learning paradigm and show that diffusion representations are effective steers during federated training. To explore the possibility of using diffusion representations in handling data heterogeneity, we propose a novel diffusion-inspired Federated paradigm with Diffusion Representation Collaboration, termed FedDifRC, leveraging meaningful guidance of diffusion models to mitigate data heterogeneity. The key idea is to construct text-driven diffusion contrasting and noise-driven diffusion regularization, aiming to provide abundant class-related semantic information and consistent convergence signals. On the one hand, we exploit the conditional feedback from the diffusion model for different text prompts to build a text-driven contrastive learning strategy. On the other hand, we introduce a noise-driven consistency regularization to align local instances with diffusion denoising representations, constraining the optimization region in the feature space. In addition, FedDifRC can be extended to a self-supervised scheme without relying on any labeled data. We also provide a theoretical analysis for FedDifRC to ensure convergence under non-convex objectives. The experiments on different scenarios validate the effectiveness of FedDifRC and the efficiency of crucial components."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 Pages, ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06482","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:31:26Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:31:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-21T01:44:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07044","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07044","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07044","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrdad","familyName":"Morsali","name":"Morsali, Mehrdad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deniz","familyName":"Najafi","name":"Najafi, Deniz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sreetama","familyName":"Sarkar","name":"Sarkar, Sreetama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pietro","familyName":"Mercati","name":"Mercati, Pietro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navid","familyName":"Khoshavi","name":"Khoshavi, Navid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Beerel","name":"Beerel, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Nikdast","name":"Nikdast, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gourav","familyName":"Datta","name":"Datta, Gourav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaahin","familyName":"Angizi","name":"Angizi, Shaahin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Opto-ViT: Architecting a Near-Sensor Region of Interest-Aware Vision Transformer Accelerator with Silicon Photonics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T17:07:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:51:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-16T03:52:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-17T00:17:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) have emerged as a powerful architecture for computer vision tasks due to their ability to model long-range dependencies and global contextual relationships. However, their substantial compute and memory demands hinder efficient deployment in scenarios with strict energy and bandwidth limitations. In this work, we propose OptoViT, the first near-sensor, region-aware ViT accelerator leveraging silicon photonics (SiPh) for real-time and energy-efficient vision processing. Opto-ViT features a hybrid electronic-photonic architecture, where the optical core handles compute-intensive matrix multiplications using Vertical-Cavity Surface-Emitting Lasers (VCSELs) and Microring Resonators (MRs), while nonlinear functions and normalization are executed electronically. To reduce redundant computation and patch processing, we introduce a lightweight Mask Generation Network (MGNet) that identifies regions of interest in the current frame and prunes irrelevant patches before ViT encoding. We further co-optimize the ViT backbone using quantization-aware training and matrix decomposition tailored for photonic constraints. Experiments across device fabrication, circuit and architecture co-design, to classification, detection, and video tasks demonstrate that OptoViT achieves 100.4 KFPS/W with up to 84% energy savings with less than 1.6% accuracy loss, while enabling scalable and efficient ViT deployment at the edge."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07044","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:45:52Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:45:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-17T02:05:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.11544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.11544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.11544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ann-Kathrin","familyName":"Dombrowski","name":"Dombrowski, Ann-Kathrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dillon","familyName":"Bowen","name":"Bowen, Dillon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Gleave","name":"Gleave, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Cundy","name":"Cundy, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Safety Gap Toolkit: Evaluating Hidden Dangers of Open-Source Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T23:58:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-17T00:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-weight large language models (LLMs) unlock huge benefits in innovation, personalization, privacy, and democratization. However, their core advantage - modifiability - opens the door to systemic risks: bad actors can trivially subvert current safeguards, turning beneficial models into tools for harm. This leads to a 'safety gap': the difference in dangerous capabilities between a model with intact safeguards and one that has been stripped of those safeguards. We open-source a toolkit to estimate the safety gap for state-of-the-art open-weight models. As a case study, we evaluate biochemical and cyber capabilities, refusal rates, and generation quality of models from two families (Llama-3 and Qwen-2.5) across a range of parameter scales (0.5B to 405B) using different safeguard removal techniques. Our experiments reveal that the safety gap widens as model scale increases and effective dangerous capabilities grow substantially when safeguards are removed. We hope that the Safety Gap Toolkit (https://github.com/AlignmentResearch/safety-gap) will serve as an evaluation framework for common open-source models and as a motivation for developing and testing tamper-resistant safeguards. We welcome contributions to the toolkit from the community."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages plus appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.11544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-17T01:28:53Z","registered":"2025-07-17T01:28:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-17T01:28:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08036","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08036","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08036","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepali","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Deepali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaklam","familyName":"Silpasuwanchai","name":"Silpasuwanchai, Chaklam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashutosh","familyName":"Modi","name":"Modi, Ashutosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhumita","familyName":"Sushil","name":"Sushil, Madhumita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sorayouth","familyName":"Chumnanvej","name":"Chumnanvej, Sorayouth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Barriers in Integrating Medical Visual Question Answering into Radiology Workflows: A Scoping Review and Clinicians' Insights"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T09:51:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:00:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T10:06:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-15T01:10:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical Visual Question Answering (MedVQA) is a promising tool to assist radiologists by automating medical image interpretation through question answering. Despite advances in models and datasets, MedVQA's integration into clinical workflows remains limited. This study systematically reviews 68 publications (2018-2024) and surveys 50 clinicians from India and Thailand to examine MedVQA's practical utility, challenges, and gaps. Following the Arksey and O'Malley scoping review framework, we used a two-pronged approach: (1) reviewing studies to identify key concepts, advancements, and research gaps in radiology workflows, and (2) surveying clinicians to capture their perspectives on MedVQA's clinical relevance. Our review reveals that nearly 60% of QA pairs are non-diagnostic and lack clinical relevance. Most datasets and models do not support multi-view, multi-resolution imaging, EHR integration, or domain knowledge, features essential for clinical diagnosis. Furthermore, there is a clear mismatch between current evaluation metrics and clinical needs. The clinician survey confirms this disconnect: only 29.8% consider MedVQA systems highly useful. Key concerns include the absence of patient history or domain knowledge (87.2%), preference for manually curated datasets (51.1%), and the need for multi-view image support (78.7%). Additionally, 66% favor models focused on specific anatomical regions, and 89.4% prefer dialogue-based interactive systems. While MedVQA shows strong potential, challenges such as limited multimodal analysis, lack of patient context, and misaligned evaluation approaches must be addressed for effective clinical integration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 5 figures (1 in supplementary), 3 tables (1 in main text, 2 in supplementary). Scoping review and clinician survey"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08036","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-14T01:13:45Z","registered":"2025-07-14T01:13:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-15T03:22:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiya","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Shiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vassilis","familyName":"Kostakos","name":"Kostakos, Vassilis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Scale: Small Language Models are Comparable to GPT-4 in Mental Health Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T02:40:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:00:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T01:10:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-15T00:55:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of Small Language Models (SLMs) as privacy-preserving alternatives for sensitive applications raises a fundamental question about their inherent understanding capabilities compared to Large Language Models (LLMs). This paper investigates the mental health understanding capabilities of current SLMs through systematic evaluation across diverse classification tasks. Employing zero-shot and few-shot learning paradigms, we benchmark their performance against established LLM baselines to elucidate their relative strengths and limitations in this critical domain. We assess five state-of-the-art SLMs (Phi-3, Phi-3.5, Qwen2.5, Llama-3.2, Gemma2) against three LLMs (GPT-4, FLAN-T5-XXL, Alpaca-7B) on six mental health understanding tasks. Our findings reveal that SLMs achieve mean performance within 2\\% of LLMs on binary classification tasks (F1 scores of 0.64 vs 0.66 in zero-shot settings), demonstrating notable competence despite orders of magnitude fewer parameters. Both model categories experience similar degradation on multi-class severity tasks (a drop of over 30\\%), suggesting that nuanced clinical understanding challenges transcend model scale. Few-shot prompting provides substantial improvements for SLMs (up to 14.6\\%), while LLM gains are more variable. Our work highlights the potential of SLMs in mental health understanding, showing they can be effective privacy-preserving tools for analyzing sensitive online text data. In particular, their ability to quickly adapt and specialize with minimal data through few-shot learning positions them as promising candidates for scalable mental health screening tools."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-14T01:13:38Z","registered":"2025-07-14T01:13:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-15T03:22:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuanning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kuanning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longfei","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Longfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyang","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Xiangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatial-Temporal Aware Visuomotor Diffusion Policy Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T10:08:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:32:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-13T06:32:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-15T00:37:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual imitation learning is effective for robots to learn versatile tasks. However, many existing methods rely on behavior cloning with supervised historical trajectories, limiting their 3D spatial and 4D spatiotemporal awareness. Consequently, these methods struggle to capture the 3D structures and 4D spatiotemporal relationships necessary for real-world deployment. In this work, we propose 4D Diffusion Policy (DP4), a novel visual imitation learning method that incorporates spatiotemporal awareness into diffusion-based policies. Unlike traditional approaches that rely on trajectory cloning, DP4 leverages a dynamic Gaussian world model to guide the learning of 3D spatial and 4D spatiotemporal perceptions from interactive environments. Our method constructs the current 3D scene from a single-view RGB-D observation and predicts the future 3D scene, optimizing trajectory generation by explicitly modeling both spatial and temporal dependencies. Extensive experiments across 17 simulation tasks with 173 variants and 3 real-world robotic tasks demonstrate that the 4D Diffusion Policy (DP4) outperforms baseline methods, improving the average simulation task success rate by 16.4% (Adroit), 14% (DexArt), and 6.45% (RLBench), and the average real-world robotic task success rate by 8.6%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:37:10Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:37:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-15T03:22:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianzhe","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Tianzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaoyan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanchi","familyName":"Ru","name":"Ru, Yanchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiping","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Haiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qika","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Qika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RAG Safety: Exploring Knowledge Poisoning Attacks to Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T13:06:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-15T00:01:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances large language models (LLMs) by retrieving external data to mitigate hallucinations and outdated knowledge issues. Benefiting from the strong ability in facilitating diverse data sources and supporting faithful reasoning, knowledge graphs (KGs) have been increasingly adopted in RAG systems, giving rise to KG-based RAG (KG-RAG) methods. Though RAG systems are widely applied in various applications, recent studies have also revealed its vulnerabilities to data poisoning attacks, where malicious information injected into external knowledge sources can mislead the system into producing incorrect or harmful responses. However, these studies focus exclusively on RAG systems using unstructured textual data sources, leaving the security risks of KG-RAG largely unexplored, despite the fact that KGs present unique vulnerabilities due to their structured and editable nature. In this work, we conduct the first systematic investigation of the security issue of KG-RAG methods through data poisoning attacks. To this end, we introduce a practical, stealthy attack setting that aligns with real-world implementation. We propose an attack strategy that first identifies adversarial target answers and then inserts perturbation triples to complete misleading inference chains in the KG, increasing the likelihood that KG-RAG methods retrieve and rely on these perturbations during generation. Through extensive experiments on two benchmarks and four recent KG-RAG methods, our attack strategy demonstrates strong effectiveness in degrading KG-RAG performance, even with minimal KG perturbations. In-depth analyses are also conducted to understand the safety threats within the internal stages of KG-RAG systems and to explore the robustness of LLMs against adversarial knowledge."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-15T02:17:38Z","registered":"2025-07-15T02:17:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-15T02:17:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sua","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyubum","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Kyubum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jung Ho","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jung Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Weighted Multi-Prompt Learning with Description-free Large Language Model Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:55:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T04:56:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in pre-trained Vision Language Models (VLM) have shown promising potential for effectively adapting to downstream tasks through prompt learning, without the need for additional annotated paired datasets. To supplement the text information in VLM trained on correlations with vision data, new approaches leveraging Large Language Models (LLM) in prompts have been proposed, enhancing robustness to unseen and diverse data. Existing methods typically extract text-based responses (i.e., descriptions) from LLM to incorporate into prompts; however, this approach suffers from high variability and low reliability. In this work, we propose Description-free Multi-prompt Learning(DeMul), a novel method that eliminates the process of extracting descriptions and instead directly distills knowledge from LLM into prompts. By adopting a description-free approach, prompts can encapsulate richer semantics while still being represented as continuous vectors for optimization, thereby eliminating the need for discrete pre-defined templates. Additionally, in a multi-prompt setting, we empirically demonstrate the potential of prompt weighting in reflecting the importance of different prompts during training. Experimental results show that our approach achieves superior performance across 11 recognition datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:33Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-14T01:42:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07137","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07137","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07137","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Yeats","name":"Yeats, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darryl","familyName":"Hannan","name":"Hannan, Darryl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Kvinge","name":"Kvinge, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Doster","name":"Doster, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Mahan","name":"Mahan, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automating Evaluation of Diffusion Model Unlearning with (Vision-) Language Model World Knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T00:51:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T05:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine unlearning (MU) is a promising cost-effective method to cleanse undesired information (generated concepts, biases, or patterns) from foundational diffusion models. While MU is orders of magnitude less costly than retraining a diffusion model without the undesired information, it can be challenging and labor-intensive to prove that the information has been fully removed from the model. Moreover, MU can damage diffusion model performance on surrounding concepts that one would like to retain, making it unclear if the diffusion model is still fit for deployment. We introduce autoeval-dmun, an automated tool which leverages (vision-) language models to thoroughly assess unlearning in diffusion models. Given a target concept, autoeval-dmun extracts structured, relevant world knowledge from the language model to identify nearby concepts which are likely damaged by unlearning and to circumvent unlearning with adversarial prompts. We use our automated tool to evaluate popular diffusion model unlearning methods, revealing that language models (1) impose semantic orderings of nearby concepts which correlate well with unlearning damage and (2) effectively circumvent unlearning with synthetic adversarial prompts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07137","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:18Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-14T01:42:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07104","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07104","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07104","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiezheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tiezheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-cheng","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Yu-cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieneng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jieneng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Yuille","name":"Yuille, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Junfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vision-Language-Vision Auto-Encoder: Scalable Knowledge Distillation from Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T17:59:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:53:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T03:43:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:14:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building state-of-the-art Vision-Language Models (VLMs) with strong captioning capabilities typically necessitates training on billions of high-quality image-text pairs, requiring millions of GPU hours. This paper introduces the Vision-Language-Vision (VLV) auto-encoder framework, which strategically leverages key pretrained components: a vision encoder, the decoder of a Text-to-Image (T2I) diffusion model, and subsequently, a Large Language Model (LLM). Specifically, we establish an information bottleneck by regularizing the language representation space, achieved through freezing the pretrained T2I diffusion decoder. Our VLV pipeline effectively distills knowledge from the text-conditioned diffusion model using continuous embeddings, demonstrating comprehensive semantic understanding via high-quality reconstructions. Furthermore, by fine-tuning a pretrained LLM to decode the intermediate language representations into detailed descriptions, we construct a state-of-the-art (SoTA) captioner comparable to leading models like GPT-4o and Gemini 2.0 Flash. Our method demonstrates exceptional cost-efficiency and significantly reduces data requirements; by primarily utilizing single-modal images for training and maximizing the utility of existing pretrained models (image encoder, T2I diffusion model, and LLM), it circumvents the need for massive paired image-text datasets, keeping the total training expenditure under $1,000 USD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://lambert-x.github.io/Vision-Language-Vision/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07104","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:47:22Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:47:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-14T01:42:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengran","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Fengran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuofeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhuofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelong","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Kelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniConv: Unifying Retrieval and Response Generation for Large Language Models in Conversations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T17:02:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:23:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of conversational search systems revolutionizes how information is accessed by enabling the multi-turn interaction between the user and the system. Existing conversational search systems are usually built with two different models. This separation restricts the system from leveraging the intrinsic knowledge of the models simultaneously, which cannot ensure the effectiveness of retrieval benefiting the generation. The existing studies for developing unified models cannot fully address the aspects of understanding conversational context, managing retrieval independently, and generating responses. In this paper, we explore how to unify dense retrieval and response generation for large language models in conversation. We conduct joint fine-tuning with different objectives and design two mechanisms to reduce the inconsistency risks while mitigating data discrepancy. The evaluations on five conversational search datasets demonstrate that our unified model can mutually improve both tasks and outperform the existing baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL 2025 (main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:45:31Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:45:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-14T01:42:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06892","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06892","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06892","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hongyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyue","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shuyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianye","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jianye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Squeeze the Soaked Sponge: Efficient Off-policy Reinforcement Finetuning for Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T14:29:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:43:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T13:42:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:36:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T10:32:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:27:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) has demonstrated its potential to improve the reasoning ability of Large Language Models (LLMs). One major limitation of most existing Reinforcement Finetuning (RFT) methods is that they are on-policy RL in nature, i.e., data generated during the past learning process is not fully utilized. This inevitably comes at a significant cost of compute and time, posing a stringent bottleneck on continuing economic and efficient scaling. To this end, we launch the renaissance of off-policy RL and propose Reincarnating Mix-policy Proximal Policy Gradient (ReMix), a general approach to enable on-policy RFT methods like PPO and GRPO to leverage off-policy data. ReMix consists of three major components: (1) Mix-policy proximal policy gradient with an increased Update-To-Data (UTD) ratio for efficient training; (2) KL-Convex policy constraint to balance the trade-off between stability and flexibility; (3) Policy reincarnation to achieve a seamless transition from efficient early-stage learning to steady asymptotic improvement. In our experiments, we train a series of ReMix models upon PPO, GRPO and 1.5B, 7B base models. ReMix shows an average Pass@1 accuracy of 52.10% (for 1.5B model) with 0.079M response rollouts, 350 training steps and achieves 63.27%/64.39% (for 7B model) with 0.007M/0.011M response rollouts, 50/75 training steps, on five math reasoning benchmarks (i.e., AIME'24, AMC'23, Minerva, OlympiadBench, and MATH500). Compared with 15 recent advanced models, ReMix shows SOTA-level performance with an over 30x to 450x reduction in training cost in terms of rollout data volume. In addition, we reveal insightful findings via multifaceted analysis, including the implicit preference for shorter responses due to the Whipping Effect of off-policy discrepancy, the collapse mode of self-reflection behavior under the presence of severe off-policyness, etc."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preliminary version, v3, added the missing name of x-axis in the left part of Fig.1 and corrected a wrong number in Fig.3. Project page: https://anitaleungxx.github.io/ReMix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06892","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:42:00Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:42:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-14T01:42:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matan","familyName":"Vetzler","name":"Vetzler, Matan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koren","familyName":"Lazar","name":"Lazar, Koren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guy","familyName":"Uziel","name":"Uziel, Guy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eran","familyName":"Hirsch","name":"Hirsch, Eran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ateret","familyName":"Anaby-Tavor","name":"Anaby-Tavor, Ateret","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leshem","familyName":"Choshen","name":"Choshen, Leshem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CRISP: Complex Reasoning with Interpretable Step-based Plans"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T11:40:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) underscore the need for stronger reasoning capabilities to solve complex problems effectively. While Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning has been a step forward, it remains insufficient for many domains. A promising alternative is explicit high-level plan generation, but existing approaches largely assume that LLMs can produce effective plans through few-shot prompting alone, without additional training. In this work, we challenge this assumption and introduce CRISP (Complex Reasoning with Interpretable Step-based Plans), a multi-domain dataset of high-level plans for mathematical reasoning and code generation. The plans in CRISP are automatically generated and rigorously validated--both intrinsically, using an LLM as a judge, and extrinsically, by evaluating their impact on downstream task performance. We demonstrate that fine-tuning a small model on CRISP enables it to generate higher-quality plans than much larger models using few-shot prompting, while significantly outperforming Chain-of-Thought reasoning. Furthermore, our out-of-domain evaluation reveals that fine-tuning on one domain improves plan generation in the other, highlighting the generalizability of learned planning capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-14T01:13:46Z","registered":"2025-07-14T01:13:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-14T01:13:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.08030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.08030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonali","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Sonali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed M.","familyName":"Alaa","name":"Alaa, Ahmed M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roxana","familyName":"Daneshjou","name":"Daneshjou, Roxana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Systematic Analysis of Declining Medical Safety Messaging in Generative AI Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T23:50:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-14T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative AI models, including large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs), are increasingly used to interpret medical images and answer clinical questions. Their responses often include inaccuracies; therefore, safety measures like medical disclaimers are critical to remind users that AI outputs are not professionally vetted or a substitute for medical advice. This study evaluated the presence of disclaimers in LLM and VLM outputs across model generations from 2022 to 2025. Using 500 mammograms, 500 chest X-rays, 500 dermatology images, and 500 medical questions, outputs were screened for disclaimer phrases. Medical disclaimer presence in LLM and VLM outputs dropped from 26.3% in 2022 to 0.97% in 2025, and from 19.6% in 2023 to 1.05% in 2025, respectively. By 2025, the majority of models displayed no disclaimers. As public models become more capable and authoritative, disclaimers must be implemented as a safeguard adapting to the clinical context of each output."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-14T01:13:36Z","registered":"2025-07-14T01:13:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-14T01:13:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06920","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06920","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06920","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taolin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Taolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maosong","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Maosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junnan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Junnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minnan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Minnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Songyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Verification for LLM Code Generation: From Generation to Testing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T14:58:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:44:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T03:12:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:12:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently achieved notable success in code-generation benchmarks such as HumanEval and LiveCodeBench. However, a detailed examination reveals that these evaluation suites often comprise only a limited number of homogeneous test cases, resulting in subtle faults going undetected. This not only artificially inflates measured performance but also compromises accurate reward estimation in reinforcement learning frameworks utilizing verifiable rewards (RLVR). To address these critical shortcomings, we systematically investigate the test-case generation (TCG) task by proposing multi-dimensional metrics designed to rigorously quantify test-suite thoroughness. Furthermore, we introduce a human-LLM collaborative method (SAGA), leveraging human programming expertise with LLM reasoning capability, aimed at significantly enhancing both the coverage and the quality of generated test cases. In addition, we develop a TCGBench to facilitate the study of the TCG task. Experiments show that SAGA achieves a detection rate of 90.62% and a verifier accuracy of 32.58% on TCGBench. The Verifier Accuracy (Verifier Acc) of the code generation evaluation benchmark synthesized by SAGA is 10.78% higher than that of LiveCodeBench-v6. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method. We hope this work contributes to building a scalable foundation for reliable LLM code evaluation, further advancing RLVR in code generation, and paving the way for automated adversarial test synthesis and adaptive benchmark integration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06920","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:42:42Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:42:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-11T02:04:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dahyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongrae","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yongrae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haeju","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Haeju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moontae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Moontae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shifting from Ranking to Set Selection for Retrieval Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T13:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:40:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T01:36:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:10:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval in Retrieval-Augmented Generation(RAG) must ensure that retrieved passages are not only individually relevant but also collectively form a comprehensive set. Existing approaches primarily rerank top-k passages based on their individual relevance, often failing to meet the information needs of complex queries in multi-hop question answering. In this work, we propose a set-wise passage selection approach and introduce SETR, which explicitly identifies the information requirements of a query through Chain-of-Thought reasoning and selects an optimal set of passages that collectively satisfy those requirements. Experiments on multi-hop RAG benchmarks show that SETR outperforms both proprietary LLM-based rerankers and open-source baselines in terms of answer correctness and retrieval quality, providing an effective and efficient alternative to traditional rerankers in RAG systems. The code is available at https://github.com/LGAI-Research/SetR"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2025 main (Oral Presentation)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:40:28Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:40:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-11T02:04:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongmeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zongmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wengang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wengang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houqiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Houqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Multimodal Large Language Models Against Modality Conflict"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T11:18:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the impressive capabilities of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) in vision-language tasks, they are prone to hallucinations in real-world scenarios. This paper investigates the hallucination phenomenon in MLLMs from the perspective of modality conflict. Unlike existing works focusing on the conflicts between model responses and inputs, we study the inherent conflicts in inputs from different modalities that place MLLMs in a dilemma and directly lead to hallucinations. We formally define the modality conflict and construct a dataset named Multimodal Modality Conflict (MMMC) to simulate this phenomenon in vision-language tasks. Three methods based on prompt engineering, supervised fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning are proposed to alleviate the hallucination caused by modality conflict. Extensive experiments are conducted on the MMMC dataset to analyze the merits and demerits of these methods. Our results show that the reinforcement learning method achieves the best performance in mitigating the hallucination under modality conflict, while the supervised fine-tuning method shows promising and stable performance. Our work sheds light on the unnoticed modality conflict that leads to hallucinations and provides more insights into the robustness of MLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:39Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-11T01:19:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunchao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xunchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Handi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Handi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoshuang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbin","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Wenbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingqing","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Qingqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dianhai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dianhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CCQ: Convolutional Code for Extreme Low-bit Quantization in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T06:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid scaling of Large Language Models (LLMs) elevates inference costs and compounds substantial deployment barriers. While quantization to 8 or 4 bits mitigates this, sub-3-bit methods face severe accuracy, scalability, and efficiency degradation. We propose Convolutional Code Quantization (CCQ), an inference-optimized quantization approach compressing LLMs to 2.0-2.75 bits with minimal accuracy loss. Departing from error-prone scalar quantization or slow vector quantization, CCQ integrates a hardware-aware bit-shift encoding and decoding solution with Convolutional Code, Hybrid Encoding, and Code Cluster, jointly overcoming accuracy-speed bottlenecks. We construct a lookup-free encoding space, enabling a linear mapping between the codebook and weight vectors, thereby optimizing inference performance. Meanwhile, by drawing on the concept of data mapping from vector quantization, we minimize the performance degradation of the model under extremely low-bit conditions. Experiments demonstrate that CCQ achieves outstanding performance on LLMs across various benchmarks. We compress DeepSeek-V3 (671B total parameters) to 184GB and ERNIE-4.5-300B-A47B to 89GB, enabling single-GPU deployment of ERNIE 4.5 and eliminating inter-card communication. The 2-bit ERNIE-4.5-300B-A47B model and inference engine have been open-sourced."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-11T01:19:30Z","registered":"2025-07-11T01:19:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-11T01:19:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vatsal","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Vatsal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Gwilliam","name":"Gwilliam, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gefen","familyName":"Kohavi","name":"Kohavi, Gefen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eshan","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Eshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Ulbricht","name":"Ulbricht, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Shrivastava","name":"Shrivastava, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Multimodal Understanding via Stable Diffusion as a Task-Aware Feature Extractor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T17:59:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:53:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have enabled image-based question-answering capabilities. However, a key limitation is the use of CLIP as the visual encoder; while it can capture coarse global information, it often can miss fine-grained details that are relevant to the input query. To address these shortcomings, this work studies whether pre-trained text-to-image diffusion models can serve as instruction-aware visual encoders. Through an analysis of their internal representations, we find diffusion features are both rich in semantics and can encode strong image-text alignment. Moreover, we find that we can leverage text conditioning to focus the model on regions relevant to the input question. We then investigate how to align these features with large language models and uncover a leakage phenomenon, where the LLM can inadvertently recover information from the original diffusion prompt. We analyze the causes of this leakage and propose a mitigation strategy. Based on these insights, we explore a simple fusion strategy that utilizes both CLIP and conditional diffusion features. We evaluate our approach on both general VQA and specialized MLLM benchmarks, demonstrating the promise of diffusion models for visual understanding, particularly in vision-centric tasks that require spatial and compositional reasoning. Our project page can be found https://vatsalag99.github.io/mustafar/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Website: see https://vatsalag99.github.io/mustafar/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:47:25Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:47:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:47:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Girella","name":"Girella, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Talon","name":"Talon, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Attribute Confusion in Fashion Text-to-Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T17:38:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:52:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the rapid advances in Text-to-Image (T2I) generation models, their evaluation remains challenging in domains like fashion, involving complex compositional generation. Recent automated T2I evaluation methods leverage pre-trained vision-language models to measure cross-modal alignment. However, our preliminary study reveals that they are still limited in assessing rich entity-attribute semantics, facing challenges in attribute confusion, i.e., when attributes are correctly depicted but associated to the wrong entities. To address this, we build on a Visual Question Answering (VQA) localization strategy targeting one single entity at a time across both visual and textual modalities. We propose a localized human evaluation protocol and introduce a novel automatic metric, Localized VQAScore (L-VQAScore), that combines visual localization with VQA probing both correct (reflection) and miss-localized (leakage) attribute generation. On a newly curated dataset featuring challenging compositional alignment scenarios, L-VQAScore outperforms state-of-the-art T2I evaluation methods in terms of correlation with human judgments, demonstrating its strength in capturing fine-grained entity-attribute associations. We believe L-VQAScore can be a reliable and scalable alternative to subjective evaluations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICIAP25. Project page: site [https://intelligolabs.github.io/L-VQAScore/\\"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:46:44Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:46:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:46:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07017","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07017","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07017","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshun","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Tianshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingshui","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Qingshui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taoran","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Taoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwei","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Xingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhoufutu","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zhoufutu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chenghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zejun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"First Return, Entropy-Eliciting Explore"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T16:45:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:50:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) improves the reasoning abilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) but it struggles with unstable exploration. We propose FR3E (First Return, Entropy-Eliciting Explore), a structured exploration framework that identifies high-uncertainty decision points in reasoning trajectories and performs targeted rollouts to construct semantically grounded intermediate feedback. Our method provides targeted guidance without relying on dense supervision. Empirical results on mathematical reasoning benchmarks(AIME24) show that FR3E promotes more stable training, produces longer and more coherent responses, and increases the proportion of fully correct trajectories. These results highlight the framework's effectiveness in improving LLM reasoning through more robust and structured exploration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07017","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:45:11Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:45:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:45:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07015","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.07015","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.07015","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Juanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MST-Distill: Mixture of Specialized Teachers for Cross-Modal Knowledge Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T16:45:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:50:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge distillation as an efficient knowledge transfer technique, has achieved remarkable success in unimodal scenarios. However, in cross-modal settings, conventional distillation methods encounter significant challenges due to data and statistical heterogeneities, failing to leverage the complementary prior knowledge embedded in cross-modal teacher models. This paper empirically reveals two critical issues in existing approaches: distillation path selection and knowledge drift. To address these limitations, we propose MST-Distill, a novel cross-modal knowledge distillation framework featuring a mixture of specialized teachers. Our approach employs a diverse ensemble of teacher models across both cross-modal and multimodal configurations, integrated with an instance-level routing network that facilitates adaptive and dynamic distillation. This architecture effectively transcends the constraints of traditional methods that rely on monotonous and static teacher models. Additionally, we introduce a plug-in masking module, independently trained to suppress modality-specific discrepancies and reconstruct teacher representations, thereby mitigating knowledge drift and enhancing transfer effectiveness. Extensive experiments across five diverse multimodal datasets, spanning visual, audio, and text, demonstrate that our method significantly outperforms existing state-of-the-art knowledge distillation methods in cross-modal distillation tasks. The source code is available at https://github.com/Gray-OREO/MST-Distill."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACM MM 2025 (The 33rd ACM International Conference on Multimedia)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07015","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:45:08Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:45:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:45:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06979","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06979","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06979","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Koromilas","name":"Koromilas, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Efthymios","familyName":"Georgiou","name":"Georgiou, Efthymios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgos","familyName":"Bouritsas","name":"Bouritsas, Giorgos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theodoros","familyName":"Giannakopoulos","name":"Giannakopoulos, Theodoros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihalis A.","familyName":"Nicolaou","name":"Nicolaou, Mihalis A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannis","familyName":"Panagakis","name":"Panagakis, Yannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Principled Framework for Multi-View Contrastive Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T16:07:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:48:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contrastive Learning (CL), a leading paradigm in Self-Supervised Learning (SSL), typically relies on pairs of data views generated through augmentation. While multiple augmentations per instance (more than two) improve generalization in supervised learning, current CL methods handle additional views suboptimally by simply aggregating different pairwise objectives. This approach suffers from four critical limitations: (L1) it utilizes multiple optimization terms per data point resulting to conflicting objectives, (L2) it fails to model all interactions across views and data points, (L3) it inherits fundamental limitations (e.g. alignment-uniformity coupling) from pairwise CL losses, and (L4) it prevents fully realizing the benefits of increased view multiplicity observed in supervised settings. We address these limitations through two novel loss functions: MV-InfoNCE, which extends InfoNCE to incorporate all possible view interactions simultaneously in one term per data point, and MV-DHEL, which decouples alignment from uniformity across views while scaling interaction complexity with view multiplicity. Both approaches are theoretically grounded - we prove they asymptotically optimize for alignment of all views and uniformity, providing principled extensions to multi-view contrastive learning. Our empirical results on ImageNet1K and three other datasets demonstrate that our methods consistently outperform existing multi-view approaches and effectively scale with increasing view multiplicity. We also apply our objectives to multimodal data and show that, in contrast to other contrastive objectives, they can scale beyond just two modalities. Most significantly, ablation studies reveal that MV-DHEL with five or more views effectively mitigates dimensionality collapse by fully utilizing the embedding space, thereby delivering multi-view benefits observed in supervised learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06979","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:44:12Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:44:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:44:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06959","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06959","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06959","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ronghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ronghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T15:40:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:46:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) are prone to hallucinations that critically compromise reliability in medical applications. While preference optimization can mitigate these hallucinations through clinical feedback, its implementation faces challenges such as clinically irrelevant training samples, imbalanced data distributions, and prohibitive expert annotation costs. To address these challenges, we introduce CheXPO, a Chest X-ray Preference Optimization strategy that combines confidence-similarity joint mining with counterfactual rationale. Our approach begins by synthesizing a unified, fine-grained multi-task chest X-ray visual instruction dataset across different question types for supervised fine-tuning (SFT). We then identify hard examples through token-level confidence analysis of SFT failures and use similarity-based retrieval to expand hard examples for balancing preference sample distributions, while synthetic counterfactual rationales provide fine-grained clinical preferences, eliminating the need for additional expert input. Experiments show that CheXPO achieves 8.93% relative performance gain using only 5% of SFT samples, reaching state-of-the-art performance across diverse clinical tasks and providing a scalable, interpretable solution for real-world radiology applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06959","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:43:42Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:43:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:43:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sezen","familyName":"Perçin","name":"Perçin, Sezen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qutub Sha","familyName":"Syed","name":"Syed, Qutub Sha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phillip","familyName":"Howard","name":"Howard, Phillip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksei","familyName":"Kuvshinov","name":"Kuvshinov, Aleksei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leo","familyName":"Schwinn","name":"Schwinn, Leo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kay-Ulrich","familyName":"Scholl","name":"Scholl, Kay-Ulrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Investigating the Robustness of Retrieval-Augmented Generation at the Query Level"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T15:39:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are very costly and inefficient to update with new information. To address this limitation, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has been proposed as a solution that dynamically incorporates external knowledge during inference, improving factual consistency and reducing hallucinations. Despite its promise, RAG systems face practical challenges-most notably, a strong dependence on the quality of the input query for accurate retrieval. In this paper, we investigate the sensitivity of different components in the RAG pipeline to various types of query perturbations. Our analysis reveals that the performance of commonly used retrievers can degrade significantly even under minor query variations. We study each module in isolation as well as their combined effect in an end-to-end question answering setting, using both general-domain and domain-specific datasets. Additionally, we propose an evaluation framework to systematically assess the query-level robustness of RAG pipelines and offer actionable recommendations for practitioners based on the results of more than 1092 experiments we performed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Generation, Evaluation \u0026amp; Metrics (GEM) Workshop at ACL 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:43:37Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:43:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:43:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06910","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06910","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06910","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fareya","familyName":"Ikram","name":"Ikram, Fareya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Scarlatos","name":"Scarlatos, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring LLMs for Predicting Tutor Strategy and Student Outcomes in Dialogues"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T14:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:44:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tutoring dialogues have gained significant attention in recent years, given the prominence of online learning and the emerging tutoring abilities of artificial intelligence (AI) agents powered by large language models (LLMs). Recent studies have shown that the strategies used by tutors can have significant effects on student outcomes, necessitating methods to predict how tutors will behave and how their actions impact students. However, few works have studied predicting tutor strategy in dialogues. Therefore, in this work we investigate the ability of modern LLMs, particularly Llama 3 and GPT-4o, to predict both future tutor moves and student outcomes in dialogues, using two math tutoring dialogue datasets. We find that even state-of-the-art LLMs struggle to predict future tutor strategy while tutor strategy is highly indicative of student outcomes, outlining a need for more powerful methods to approach this task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in BEA 2025: 20th Workshop on Innovative Use of NLP for Building Educational Applications"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06910","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:42:26Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:42:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:42:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06895","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06895","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06895","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Mariotti","name":"Mariotti, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronica","familyName":"Guidetti","name":"Guidetti, Veronica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federica","familyName":"Mandreoli","name":"Mandreoli, Federica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SCoRE: Streamlined Corpus-based Relation Extraction using Multi-Label Contrastive Learning and Bayesian kNN"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T14:33:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:43:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing demand for efficient knowledge graph (KG) enrichment leveraging external corpora has intensified interest in relation extraction (RE), particularly under low-supervision settings. To address the need for adaptable and noise-resilient RE solutions that integrate seamlessly with pre-trained large language models (PLMs), we introduce SCoRE, a modular and cost-effective sentence-level RE system. SCoRE enables easy PLM switching, requires no finetuning, and adapts smoothly to diverse corpora and KGs. By combining supervised contrastive learning with a Bayesian k-Nearest Neighbors (kNN) classifier for multi-label classification, it delivers robust performance despite the noisy annotations of distantly supervised corpora. To improve RE evaluation, we propose two novel metrics: Correlation Structure Distance (CSD), measuring the alignment between learned relational patterns and KG structures, and Precision at R (P@R), assessing utility as a recommender system. We also release Wiki20d, a benchmark dataset replicating real-world RE conditions where only KG-derived annotations are available. Experiments on five benchmarks show that SCoRE matches or surpasses state-of-the-art methods while significantly reducing energy consumption. Further analyses reveal that increasing model complexity, as seen in prior work, degrades performance, highlighting the advantages of SCoRE's minimal design. Combining efficiency, modularity, and scalability, SCoRE stands as an optimal choice for real-world RE applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06895","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:42:04Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:42:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:42:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06893","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06893","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06893","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Abbas","name":"Abbas, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Celia","familyName":"Waggoner","name":"Waggoner, Celia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Olive","name":"Olive, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Developing and Maintaining an Open-Source Repository of AI Evaluations: Challenges and Insights"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T14:30:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:43:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI evaluations have become critical tools for assessing large language model capabilities and safety. This paper presents practical insights from eight months of maintaining $inspect\\_evals$, an open-source repository of 70+ community-contributed AI evaluations. We identify key challenges in implementing and maintaining AI evaluations and develop solutions including: (1) a structured cohort management framework for scaling community contributions, (2) statistical methodologies for optimal resampling and cross-model comparison with uncertainty quantification, and (3) systematic quality control processes for reproducibility. Our analysis reveals that AI evaluation requires specialized infrastructure, statistical rigor, and community coordination beyond traditional software development practices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06893","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:42:01Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:42:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:42:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06830","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06830","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06830","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianbing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xianbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tien Tsin","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Tien Tsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Rezatofighi","name":"Rezatofighi, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gholamreza","familyName":"Haffari","name":"Haffari, Gholamreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhen","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Lizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-Grounded Motion Forecasting via Equation Discovery for Trajectory-Guided Image-to-Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T13:28:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:40:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in diffusion-based and autoregressive video generation models have achieved remarkable visual realism. However, these models typically lack accurate physical alignment, failing to replicate real-world dynamics in object motion. This limitation arises primarily from their reliance on learned statistical correlations rather than capturing mechanisms adhering to physical laws. To address this issue, we introduce a novel framework that integrates symbolic regression (SR) and trajectory-guided image-to-video (I2V) models for physics-grounded video forecasting. Our approach extracts motion trajectories from input videos, uses a retrieval-based pre-training mechanism to enhance symbolic regression, and discovers equations of motion to forecast physically accurate future trajectories. These trajectories then guide video generation without requiring fine-tuning of existing models. Evaluated on scenarios in Classical Mechanics, including spring-mass, pendulums, and projectile motions, our method successfully recovers ground-truth analytical equations and improves the physical alignment of generated videos over baseline methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06830","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:40:16Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:40:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:40:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zenan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexuan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Zexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanhua","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Guanhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Siheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kejiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengzong","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Fengzong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanhui","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Zhanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Termination for Multi-round Parallel Reasoning: An Universal Semantic Entropy-Guided Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T13:28:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:40:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have accelerated progress toward artificial general intelligence, with inference-time scaling emerging as a key technique. Contemporary approaches leverage either sequential reasoning (iteratively extending chains of thought) or parallel reasoning (generating multiple solutions simultaneously) to scale inference. However, both paradigms face fundamental limitations: sequential scaling typically relies on arbitrary token budgets for termination, leading to inefficiency or premature cutoff; while parallel scaling often lacks coordination among parallel branches and requires intrusive fine-tuning to perform effectively. In light of these challenges, we aim to design a flexible test-time collaborative inference framework that exploits the complementary strengths of both sequential and parallel reasoning paradigms. Towards this goal, the core challenge lies in developing an efficient and accurate intrinsic quality metric to assess model responses during collaborative inference, enabling dynamic control and early termination of the reasoning trace. To address this challenge, we introduce semantic entropy (SE), which quantifies the semantic diversity of parallel model responses and serves as a robust indicator of reasoning quality due to its strong negative correlation with accuracy..."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 5 fiures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:40:15Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:40:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:40:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06802","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06802","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06802","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonjin","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Wonjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungil","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Sungil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong-Yeon","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Dong-Yeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Speech Tokenizer is Key to Consistent Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T12:43:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:38:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech tokenization is crucial in digital speech processing, converting continuous speech signals into discrete units for various computational tasks. This paper introduces a novel speech tokenizer with broad applicability across downstream tasks. While recent advances in residual vector quantization (RVQ) have incorporated semantic elements, they often neglect critical acoustic features. We propose an advanced approach that simultaneously encodes both linguistic and acoustic information, preserving prosodic and emotional content. Our method significantly enhances speech representation fidelity across diverse applications. Empirical evaluations demonstrate its effectiveness in speech coding, voice conversion, emotion recognition, and multimodal language modeling, without requiring additional training. This versatility underscores its potential as a key tool for advancing AI-driven speech processing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06802","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:39:29Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:39:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:39:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan Hon Michael","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Yan Hon Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghyeok","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Donghyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finetuning Vision-Language Models as OCR Systems for Low-Resource Languages: A Case Study of Manchu"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T11:38:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:35:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Manchu, a critically endangered language essential for understanding early modern Eastern Eurasian history, lacks effective OCR systems that can handle real-world historical documents. This study develops high-performing OCR systems by fine-tuning three open-source vision-language models (LLaMA-3.2-11B, Qwen2.5-VL-7B, Qwen2.5-VL-3B) on 60,000 synthetic Manchu word images using parameter-efficient training. LLaMA-3.2-11B achieved exceptional performance with 98.3\\% word accuracy and 0.0024 character error rate on synthetic data, while crucially maintaining 93.1\\% accuracy on real-world handwritten documents. Comparative evaluation reveals substantial advantages over traditional approaches: while a CRNN baseline achieved 99.8\\% synthetic accuracy, it suffered severe degradation to 72.5\\% on real documents. Our approach demonstrates effective synthetic-to-real domain transfer, providing a cost-effective solution deployable on accessible infrastructure. This work establishes a transferable framework for endangered language OCR that removes technical and financial barriers in digital humanities, enabling historians and linguists to process historical archives without specialized computing resources. Code and model weights are available at https://github.com/mic7ch1/ManchuAI-OCR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:38:28Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:38:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:38:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06739","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06739","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06739","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyuan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Mengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PromptTea: Let Prompts Tell TeaCache the Optimal Threshold"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T10:53:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:34:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent progress in video generation, inference speed remains a major bottleneck. A common acceleration strategy involves reusing model outputs via caching mechanisms at fixed intervals. However, we find that such fixed-frequency reuse significantly degrades quality in complex scenes, while manually tuning reuse thresholds is inefficient and lacks robustness. To address this, we propose Prompt-Complexity-Aware (PCA) caching, a method that automatically adjusts reuse thresholds based on scene complexity estimated directly from the input prompt. By incorporating prompt-derived semantic cues, PCA enables more adaptive and informed reuse decisions than conventional caching methods. We also revisit the assumptions behind TeaCache and identify a key limitation: it suffers from poor input-output relationship modeling due to an oversimplified prior. To overcome this, we decouple the noisy input, enhance the contribution of meaningful textual information, and improve the model's predictive accuracy through multivariate polynomial feature expansion. To further reduce computational cost, we replace the static CFGCache with DynCFGCache, a dynamic mechanism that selectively reuses classifier-free guidance (CFG) outputs based on estimated output variations. This allows for more flexible reuse without compromising output quality. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach achieves significant acceleration-for example, 2.79x speedup on the Wan2.1 model-while maintaining high visual fidelity across a range of scenes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06739","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:37:55Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:37:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:37:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06679","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06679","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06679","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miaojing","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Miaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cairong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Cairong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T09:12:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:29:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large vision-language models (VLMs) have shown remarkable progress in solving the text-promptable object counting problem. Representative methods typically specify text prompts with object category information in images. This however is insufficient for training the model to accurately distinguish the number of objects in the counting task. To this end, we propose QUANet, which introduces novel quantity-oriented text prompts with a vision-text quantity alignment loss to enhance the model's quantity awareness. Moreover, we propose a dual-stream adaptive counting decoder consisting of a Transformer stream, a CNN stream, and a number of Transformer-to-CNN enhancement adapters (T2C-adapters) for density map prediction. The T2C-adapters facilitate the effective knowledge communication and aggregation between the Transformer and CNN streams. A cross-stream quantity ranking loss is proposed in the end to optimize the ranking orders of predictions from the two streams. Extensive experiments on standard benchmarks such as FSC-147, CARPK, PUCPR+, and ShanghaiTech demonstrate our model's strong generalizability for zero-shot class-agnostic counting. Code is available at https://github.com/viscom-tongji/QUANet"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06679","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:36:24Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:36:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:36:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Jaw","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wei-Jaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang-Chih","familyName":"Hsieh","name":"Hsieh, Fang-Chih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanjun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang-Duo","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Fang-Duo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Hsuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi-Hsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring State-Space-Model based Language Model in Music Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T09:05:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:29:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The recent surge in State Space Models (SSMs), particularly the emergence of Mamba, has established them as strong alternatives or complementary modules to Transformers across diverse domains. In this work, we aim to explore the potential of Mamba-based architectures for text-to-music generation. We adopt discrete tokens of Residual Vector Quantization (RVQ) as the modeling representation and empirically find that a single-layer codebook can capture semantic information in music. Motivated by this observation, we focus on modeling a single-codebook representation and adapt SiMBA, originally designed as a Mamba-based encoder, to function as a decoder for sequence modeling. We compare its performance against a standard Transformer-based decoder. Our results suggest that, under limited-resource settings, SiMBA achieves much faster convergence and generates outputs closer to the ground truth. This demonstrates the promise of SSMs for efficient and expressive text-to-music generation. We put audio examples on Github."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ISMIR 2025 as Late-Breaking Demo (LBD)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:36:16Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:36:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:36:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06651","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06651","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06651","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juncheng","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Juncheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Ping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiao-Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diff$^2$I2P: Differentiable Image-to-Point Cloud Registration with Diffusion Prior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T08:30:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:28:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning cross-modal correspondences is essential for image-to-point cloud (I2P) registration. Existing methods achieve this mostly by utilizing metric learning to enforce feature alignment across modalities, disregarding the inherent modality gap between image and point data. Consequently, this paradigm struggles to ensure accurate cross-modal correspondences. To this end, inspired by the cross-modal generation success of recent large diffusion models, we propose Diff$^2$I2P, a fully Differentiable I2P registration framework, leveraging a novel and effective Diffusion prior for bridging the modality gap. Specifically, we propose a Control-Side Score Distillation (CSD) technique to distill knowledge from a depth-conditioned diffusion model to directly optimize the predicted transformation. However, the gradients on the transformation fail to backpropagate onto the cross-modal features due to the non-differentiability of correspondence retrieval and PnP solver. To this end, we further propose a Deformable Correspondence Tuning (DCT) module to estimate the correspondences in a differentiable way, followed by the transformation estimation using a differentiable PnP solver. With these two designs, the Diffusion model serves as a strong prior to guide the cross-modal feature learning of image and point cloud for forming robust correspondences, which significantly improves the registration. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that Diff$^2$I2P consistently outperforms SoTA I2P registration methods, achieving over 7% improvement in registration recall on the 7-Scenes benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06651","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:35:41Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:35:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:35:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06623","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06623","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06623","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Stewart-Evans","name":"Stewart-Evans, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tessa","familyName":"Langley","name":"Langley, Tessa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Prayle","name":"Prayle, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela","familyName":"Hands","name":"Hands, Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karen","familyName":"Exley","name":"Exley, Karen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jo","familyName":"Leonardi-Bee","name":"Leonardi-Bee, Jo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Expediting data extraction using a large language model (LLM) and scoping review protocol: a methodological study within a complex scoping review"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:50:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:26:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The data extraction stages of reviews are resource-intensive, and researchers may seek to expediate data extraction using online (large language models) LLMs and review protocols. Claude 3.5 Sonnet was used to trial two approaches that used a review protocol to prompt data extraction from 10 evidence sources included in a case study scoping review. A protocol-based approach was also used to review extracted data. Limited performance evaluation was undertaken which found high accuracy for the two extraction approaches (83.3% and 100%) when extracting simple, well-defined citation details; accuracy was lower (9.6% and 15.8%) when extracting more complex, subjective data items. Considering all data items, both approaches had precision \u0026gt;90% but low recall (\u0026lt;25%) and F1 scores (\u0026lt;40%). The context of a complex scoping review, open response types and methodological approach likely impacted performance due to missed and misattributed data. LLM feedback considered the baseline extraction accurate and suggested minor amendments: four of 15 (26.7%) to citation details and 8 of 38 (21.1%) to key findings data items were considered to potentially add value. However, when repeating the process with a dataset featuring deliberate errors, only 2 of 39 (5%) errors were detected. Review-protocol-based methods used for expediency require more robust performance evaluation across a range of LLMs and review contexts with comparison to conventional prompt engineering approaches. We recommend researchers evaluate and report LLM performance if using them similarly to conduct data extraction or review extracted data. LLM feedback contributed to protocol adaptation and may assist future review protocol drafting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06623","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:35:00Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:35:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:35:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boshko","familyName":"Koloski","name":"Koloski, Boshko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senja","familyName":"Pollak","name":"Pollak, Senja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Navigli","name":"Navigli, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Blaž","familyName":"Škrlj","name":"Škrlj, Blaž","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FuDoBa: Fusing Document and Knowledge Graph-based Representations with Bayesian Optimisation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:49:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:26:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building on the success of Large Language Models (LLMs), LLM-based representations have dominated the document representation landscape, achieving great performance on the document embedding benchmarks. However, the high-dimensional, computationally expensive embeddings from LLMs tend to be either too generic or inefficient for domain-specific applications. To address these limitations, we introduce FuDoBa a Bayesian optimisation-based method that integrates LLM-based embeddings with domain-specific structured knowledge, sourced both locally and from external repositories like WikiData. This fusion produces low-dimensional, task-relevant representations while reducing training complexity and yielding interpretable early-fusion weights for enhanced classification performance. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach on six datasets in two domains, showing that when paired with robust AutoML-based classifiers, our proposed representation learning approach performs on par with, or surpasses, those produced solely by the proprietary LLM-based embedding baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:34:59Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:34:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:34:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06613","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06613","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06613","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anshuk","familyName":"Uppal","name":"Uppal, Anshuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhta","familyName":"Takida","name":"Takida, Yuhta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chieh-Hsin","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Chieh-Hsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Denoising Multi-Beta VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:29:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:25:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Disentangled and interpretable latent representations in generative models typically come at the cost of generation quality. The $β$-VAE framework introduces a hyperparameter $β$ to balance disentanglement and reconstruction quality, where setting $β\u0026gt; 1$ introduces an information bottleneck that favors disentanglement over sharp, accurate reconstructions. To address this trade-off, we propose a novel generative modeling framework that leverages a range of $β$ values to learn multiple corresponding latent representations. First, we obtain a slew of representations by training a single variational autoencoder (VAE), with a new loss function that controls the information retained in each latent representation such that the higher $β$ value prioritize disentanglement over reconstruction fidelity. We then, introduce a non-linear diffusion model that smoothly transitions latent representations corresponding to different $β$ values. This model denoises towards less disentangled and more informative representations, ultimately leading to (almost) lossless representations, enabling sharp reconstructions. Furthermore, our model supports sample generation without input images, functioning as a standalone generative model. We evaluate our framework in terms of both disentanglement and generation quality. Additionally, we observe smooth transitions in the latent spaces with respect to changes in $β$, facilitating consistent manipulation of generated outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 8 figures and 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06613","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:34:45Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:34:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:34:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"li","name":"li, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiying","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Zhiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederick W. B.","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Frederick W. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohui","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MOST: Motion Diffusion Model for Rare Text via Temporal Clip Banzhaf Interaction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T06:51:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce MOST, a novel motion diffusion model via temporal clip Banzhaf interaction, aimed at addressing the persistent challenge of generating human motion from rare language prompts. While previous approaches struggle with coarse-grained matching and overlook important semantic cues due to motion redundancy, our key insight lies in leveraging fine-grained clip relationships to mitigate these issues. MOST's retrieval stage presents the first formulation of its kind - temporal clip Banzhaf interaction - which precisely quantifies textual-motion coherence at the clip level. This facilitates direct, fine-grained text-to-motion clip matching and eliminates prevalent redundancy. In the generation stage, a motion prompt module effectively utilizes retrieved motion clips to produce semantically consistent movements. Extensive evaluations confirm that MOST achieves state-of-the-art text-to-motion retrieval and generation performance by comprehensively addressing previous challenges, as demonstrated through quantitative and qualitative results highlighting its effectiveness, especially for rare prompts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:34:09Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:34:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:34:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinjie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minpeng","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Minpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengxi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Biao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinggao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinggao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Data-Centric to Sample-Centric: Enhancing LLM Reasoning via Progressive Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T06:05:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:22:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) has recently advanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). While prior work has emphasized algorithmic design, data curation, and reward shaping, we investigate RLVR from a sample-centric perspective and introduce LPPO (Learning-Progress and Prefix-guided Optimization), a framework of progressive optimization techniques. Our work addresses a critical question: how to best leverage a small set of trusted, high-quality demonstrations, rather than simply scaling up data volume. First, motivated by how hints aid human problem-solving, we propose prefix-guided sampling, an online data augmentation method that incorporates partial solution prefixes from expert demonstrations to guide the policy, particularly for challenging instances. Second, inspired by how humans focus on important questions aligned with their current capabilities, we introduce learning-progress weighting, a dynamic strategy that adjusts each training sample's influence based on model progression. We estimate sample-level learning progress via an exponential moving average of per-sample pass rates, promoting samples that foster learning and de-emphasizing stagnant ones. Experiments on mathematical-reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that our methods outperform strong baselines, yielding faster convergence and a higher performance ceiling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:33:44Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:33:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:33:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Huai","name":"Huai, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yichun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xinhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SkyVLN: Vision-and-Language Navigation and NMPC Control for UAVs in Urban Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T05:38:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:21:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) have emerged as versatile tools across various sectors, driven by their mobility and adaptability. This paper introduces SkyVLN, a novel framework integrating vision-and-language navigation (VLN) with Nonlinear Model Predictive Control (NMPC) to enhance UAV autonomy in complex urban environments. Unlike traditional navigation methods, SkyVLN leverages Large Language Models (LLMs) to interpret natural language instructions and visual observations, enabling UAVs to navigate through dynamic 3D spaces with improved accuracy and robustness. We present a multimodal navigation agent equipped with a fine-grained spatial verbalizer and a history path memory mechanism. These components allow the UAV to disambiguate spatial contexts, handle ambiguous instructions, and backtrack when necessary. The framework also incorporates an NMPC module for dynamic obstacle avoidance, ensuring precise trajectory tracking and collision prevention. To validate our approach, we developed a high-fidelity 3D urban simulation environment using AirSim, featuring realistic imagery and dynamic urban elements. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SkyVLN significantly improves navigation success rates and efficiency, particularly in new and unseen environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 9 figures, has been accepted by IROS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:33:31Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:33:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:33:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06528","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06528","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06528","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huisheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoshi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhuoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangjing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hangjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H. Vicky","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, H. Vicky","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InvestAlign: Overcoming Data Scarcity in Aligning Large Language Models with Investor Decision-Making Processes under Herd Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T04:07:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:18:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning Large Language Models (LLMs) with investor decision-making processes under herd behavior is a critical challenge in behavioral finance, which grapples with a fundamental limitation: the scarcity of real-user data needed for Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT). While SFT can bridge the gap between LLM outputs and human behavioral patterns, its reliance on massive authentic data imposes substantial collection costs and privacy risks. We propose InvestAlign, a novel framework that constructs high-quality SFT datasets by leveraging theoretical solutions to similar and simple optimal investment problems rather than complex scenarios. Our theoretical analysis demonstrates that training LLMs with InvestAlign-generated data achieves faster parameter convergence than using real-user data, suggesting superior learning efficiency. Furthermore, we develop InvestAgent, an LLM agent fine-tuned with InvestAlign, which demonstrates significantly closer alignment to real-user data than pre-SFT models in both simple and complex investment problems. This highlights our proposed InvestAlign as a promising approach with the potential to address complex optimal investment problems and align LLMs with investor decision-making processes under herd behavior. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/thu-social-network-research-group/InvestAlign."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06528","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:32:36Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:32:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:32:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06523","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06523","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06523","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqiang","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Liqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viet","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Viet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyun","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Seunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinya","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FIFA: Unified Faithfulness Evaluation Framework for Text-to-Video and Video-to-Text Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T03:51:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:17:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Multimodal Large Language Models (VideoMLLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in both Video-to-Text and Text-to-Video tasks. However, they often suffer fro hallucinations, generating content that contradicts the visual input. Existing evaluation methods are limited to one task (e.g., V2T) and also fail to assess hallucinations in open-ended, free-form responses. To address this gap, we propose FIFA, a unified FaIthFulness evAluation framework that extracts comprehensive descriptive facts, models their semantic dependencies via a Spatio-Temporal Semantic Dependency Graph, and verifies them using VideoQA models. We further introduce Post-Correction, a tool-based correction framework that revises hallucinated content. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FIFA aligns more closely with human judgment than existing evaluation methods, and that Post-Correction effectively improves factual consistency in both text and video generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06523","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:32:29Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:32:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:32:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06516","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06516","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06516","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunrui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gustavo","familyName":"Batista","name":"Batista, Gustavo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salil S.","familyName":"Kanhere","name":"Kanhere, Salil S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Instance-Wise Monotonic Calibration by Constrained Transformation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T03:32:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep neural networks often produce miscalibrated probability estimates, leading to overconfident predictions. A common approach for calibration is fitting a post-hoc calibration map on unseen validation data that transforms predicted probabilities. A key desirable property of the calibration map is instance-wise monotonicity (i.e., preserving the ranking of probability outputs). However, most existing post-hoc calibration methods do not guarantee monotonicity. Previous monotonic approaches either use an under-parameterized calibration map with limited expressive ability or rely on black-box neural networks, which lack interpretability and robustness. In this paper, we propose a family of novel monotonic post-hoc calibration methods, which employs a constrained calibration map parameterized linearly with respect to the number of classes. Our proposed approach ensures expressiveness, robustness, and interpretability while preserving the relative ordering of the probability output by formulating the proposed calibration map as a constrained optimization problem. Our proposed methods achieve state-of-the-art performance across datasets with different deep neural network models, outperforming existing calibration methods while being data and computation-efficient. Our code is available at https://github.com/YunruiZhang/Calibration-by-Constrained-Transformation"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Conference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence (UAI)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06516","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:32:18Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:32:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:32:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06510","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06510","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06510","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changxing","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Changxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoli","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shaoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangmin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models for Open Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T03:16:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open vocabulary Human-Object Interaction (HOI) detection is a challenging task that detects all triplets of interest in an image, even those that are not pre-defined in the training set. Existing approaches typically rely on output features generated by large Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to enhance the generalization ability of interaction representations. However, the visual features produced by VLMs are holistic and coarse-grained, which contradicts the nature of detection tasks. To address this issue, we propose a novel Bilateral Collaboration framework for open vocabulary HOI detection (BC-HOI). This framework includes an Attention Bias Guidance (ABG) component, which guides the VLM to produce fine-grained instance-level interaction features according to the attention bias provided by the HOI detector. It also includes a Large Language Model (LLM)-based Supervision Guidance (LSG) component, which provides fine-grained token-level supervision for the HOI detector by the LLM component of the VLM. LSG enhances the ability of ABG to generate high-quality attention bias. We conduct extensive experiments on two popular benchmarks: HICO-DET and V-COCO, consistently achieving superior performance in the open vocabulary and closed settings. The code will be released in Github."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06510","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:32:09Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:32:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:32:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06466","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06466","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06466","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Dharna","name":"Dharna, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Clune","name":"Clune, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Foundation Model Self-Play: Open-Ended Strategy Innovation via Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T00:58:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:11:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent interactions have long fueled innovation, from natural predator-prey dynamics to the space race. Self-play (SP) algorithms try to harness these dynamics by pitting agents against ever-improving opponents, thereby creating an implicit curriculum toward learning high-quality solutions. However, SP often fails to produce diverse solutions and can get stuck in locally optimal behaviors. We introduce Foundation-Model Self-Play (FMSP), a new direction that leverages the code-generation capabilities and vast knowledge of foundation models (FMs) to overcome these challenges by leaping across local optima in policy space. We propose a family of approaches: (1) \\textbf{Vanilla Foundation-Model Self-Play (vFMSP)} continually refines agent policies via competitive self-play; (2) \\textbf{Novelty-Search Self-Play (NSSP)} builds a diverse population of strategies, ignoring performance; and (3) the most promising variant, \\textbf{Quality-Diveristy Self-Play (QDSP)}, creates a diverse set of high-quality policies by combining the diversity of NSSP and refinement of vFMSP. We evaluate FMSPs in Car Tag, a continuous-control pursuer-evader setting, and in Gandalf, a simple AI safety simulation in which an attacker tries to jailbreak an LLM's defenses. In Car Tag, FMSPs explore a wide variety of reinforcement learning, tree search, and heuristic-based methods, to name just a few. In terms of discovered policy quality, \\ouralgo and vFMSP surpass strong human-designed strategies. In Gandalf, FMSPs can successfully automatically red-team an LLM, breaking through and jailbreaking six different, progressively stronger levels of defense. Furthermore, FMSPs can automatically proceed to patch the discovered vulnerabilities. Overall, FMSPs represent a promising new research frontier of improving self-play with foundation models, opening fresh paths toward more creative and open-ended strategy discovery"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"67 pages, accepted to RLC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06466","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:31:01Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:31:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:31:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06461","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06461","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06461","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Risi","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Risi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Supriyo","familyName":"Datta","name":"Datta, Supriyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph G.","familyName":"Makin","name":"Makin, Joseph G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with a Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T00:29:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:33:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reducing energy consumption has become a pressing need for modern machine learning, which has achieved many of its most impressive results by scaling to larger and more energy-consumptive neural networks. Unfortunately, the main algorithm for training such networks, backpropagation, poses significant challenges for custom hardware accelerators, due to both its serial dependencies and the memory footprint needed to store forward activations for the backward pass. Alternatives to backprop, although less effective, do exist; here the main computational bottleneck becomes matrix multiplication. In this study, we derive forward-forward algorithms for binary, stochastic units. Binarization of the activations transforms matrix multiplications into indexing operations, which can be executed efficiently in hardware. Stochasticity, combined with tied weights across units with different biases, bypasses the information bottleneck imposed by binary units. Furthermore, although slow and expensive in traditional hardware, binary sampling that is very fast can be implemented cheaply with p-bits (probabilistic bits), novel devices made up of unstable magnets. We evaluate our proposed algorithms on the MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 datasets, showing that its performance is close to real-valued forward-forward, but with an estimated energy savings of about one order of magnitude."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 5 figures, 4 tables. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06461","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:30:54Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:30:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:30:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyu","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Qianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Anagnostopoulos","name":"Anagnostopoulos, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daning","familyName":"Bi","name":"Bi, Daning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedPhD: Federated Pruning with Hierarchical Learning of Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.11; C.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05, 68T07, 68Q85, 94A08","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T23:24:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:09:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated Learning (FL), as a distributed learning paradigm, trains models over distributed clients' data. FL is particularly beneficial for distributed training of Diffusion Models (DMs), which are high-quality image generators that require diverse data. However, challenges such as high communication costs and data heterogeneity persist in training DMs similar to training Transformers and Convolutional Neural Networks. Limited research has addressed these issues in FL environments. To address this gap and challenges, we introduce a novel approach, FedPhD, designed to efficiently train DMs in FL environments. FedPhD leverages Hierarchical FL with homogeneity-aware model aggregation and selection policy to tackle data heterogeneity while reducing communication costs. The distributed structured pruning of FedPhD enhances computational efficiency and reduces model storage requirements in clients. Our experiments across multiple datasets demonstrate that FedPhD achieves high model performance regarding Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) scores while reducing communication costs by up to $88\\%$. FedPhD outperforms baseline methods achieving at least a $34\\%$ improvement in FID, while utilizing only $56\\%$ of the total computation and communication resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 8 figures, 5 tables. This paper introduces FedPhD, a novel hierarchical federated learning framework for training diffusion models that addresses data heterogeneity and communication costs through homogeneity-aware aggregation and structured pruning. Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Cybernetics and is under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:30:36Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:30:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:30:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06427","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06427","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06427","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Loakman","name":"Loakman, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youbo","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Youbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuting","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yuting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chenghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Task Performance with Interpretable Models via Sparse Auto-Encoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T22:17:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are traditionally viewed as black-box algorithms, therefore reducing trustworthiness and obscuring potential approaches to increasing performance on downstream tasks. In this work, we apply an effective LLM decomposition method using a dictionary-learning approach with sparse autoencoders. This helps extract monosemantic features from polysemantic LLM neurons. Remarkably, our work identifies model-internal misunderstanding, allowing the automatic reformulation of the prompts with additional annotations to improve the interpretation by LLMs. Moreover, this approach demonstrates a significant performance improvement in downstream tasks, such as mathematical reasoning and metaphor detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06427","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:30:03Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:30:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:30:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mandana","familyName":"Vaziri","name":"Vaziri, Mandana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Mandel","name":"Mandel, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hirokuni","familyName":"Kitahara","name":"Kitahara, Hirokuni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Hirzel","name":"Hirzel, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anca","familyName":"Sailer","name":"Sailer, Anca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Representing Prompting Patterns with PDL: Compliance Agent Case Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T21:03:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:06:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt engineering for LLMs remains complex, with existing frameworks either hiding complexity behind restrictive APIs or providing inflexible canned patterns that resist customization -- making sophisticated agentic programming challenging. We present the Prompt Declaration Language (PDL), a novel approach to prompt representation that tackles this fundamental complexity by bringing prompts to the forefront, enabling manual and automatic prompt tuning while capturing the composition of LLM calls together with rule-based code and external tools. By abstracting away the plumbing for such compositions, PDL aims at improving programmer productivity while providing a declarative representation that is amenable to optimization. This paper demonstrates PDL's utility through a real-world case study of a compliance agent. Tuning the prompting pattern of this agent yielded up to 4x performance improvement compared to using a canned agent and prompt pattern."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2025 Workshop on Programmatic Representations for Agent Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:29:18Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:29:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:29:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06378","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06378","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06378","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine","familyName":"Arnett","name":"Arnett, Catherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marisa","familyName":"Hudspeth","name":"Hudspeth, Marisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brendan","familyName":"O'Connor","name":"O'Connor, Brendan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Morphological Alignment of Tokenizers in 70 Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T20:32:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While tokenization is a key step in language modeling, with effects on model training and performance, it remains unclear how to effectively evaluate tokenizer quality. One proposed dimension of tokenizer quality is the extent to which tokenizers preserve linguistically meaningful subwords, aligning token boundaries with morphological boundaries within a word. We expand MorphScore (Arnett \u0026amp; Bergen, 2025), which previously covered 22 languages, to support a total of 70 languages. The updated MorphScore offers more flexibility in evaluation and addresses some of the limitations of the original version. We then correlate our alignment scores with downstream task performance for five pre-trained languages models on seven tasks, with at least one task in each of the languages in our sample. We find that morphological alignment does not explain very much variance in model performance, suggesting that morphological alignment alone does not measure dimensions of tokenization quality relevant to model performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures. Accepted to the Tokenization Workshop at ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06378","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:28:52Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:28:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:28:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06376","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06376","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06376","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisavet Lydia","familyName":"Alvanaki","name":"Alvanaki, Elisavet Lydia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca P.","familyName":"Carloni","name":"Carloni, Luca P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SLDB: An End-To-End Heterogeneous System-on-Chip Benchmark Suite for LLM-Aided Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T20:27:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Over the last few years, Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a valuable tool for Electronic Design Automation (EDA). State-of-the-art research in LLM-aided design has demonstrated the ability of LLMs to generate syntactically correct RTL code, showcasing encouraging prospects for integrating AI into the hardware design process. A key enabler of these advancements is the availability of high-quality benchmarks to evaluate new approaches. However, existing datasets and benchmarks fall short of system-level design, as they focus primarily on component-level information and low-complexity designs. To address this gap, we introduce the System-Level Design Benchmark (SLDB), a dataset tailored for evaluating LLMs in system-level integration and configuration tasks. SLDB includes a curated benchmark suite of 10 baseline SoC designs, whose components can be combined into an exponential number of distinct tile-based SoCs through a synthetic library. The dataset provides full SoC configurations, accelerator integration code, communication parameters, and accelerator-aware system configurations, along with testing-application code, compatible with the ESP platform[1]."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06376","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:28:49Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:28:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:28:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06335","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06335","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06335","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Casey","familyName":"Kennington","name":"Kennington, Casey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Schlangen","name":"Schlangen, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Could the Road to Grounded, Neuro-symbolic AI be Paved with Words-as-Classifiers?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T18:44:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:03:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Formal, Distributional, and Grounded theories of computational semantics each have their uses and their drawbacks. There has been a shift to ground models of language by adding visual knowledge, and there has been a call to enrich models of language with symbolic methods to gain the benefits from formal, distributional, and grounded theories. In this paper, we attempt to make the case that one potential path forward in unifying all three semantic fields is paved with the words-as-classifier model, a model of word-level grounded semantics that has been incorporated into formalisms and distributional language models in the literature, and it has been well-tested within interactive dialogue settings. We review that literature, motivate the words-as-classifiers model with an appeal to recent work in cognitive science, and describe a small experiment. Finally, we sketch a model of semantics unified through words-as-classifiers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06335","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:27:47Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:27:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:27:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06323","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06323","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06323","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarek","familyName":"Gasmi","name":"Gasmi, Tarek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramzi","familyName":"Guesmi","name":"Guesmi, Ramzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ines","familyName":"Belhadj","name":"Belhadj, Ines","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihene","familyName":"Bennaceur","name":"Bennaceur, Jihene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging AI and Software Security: A Comparative Vulnerability Assessment of LLM Agent Deployment Paradigms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T18:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:02:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) agents face security vulnerabilities spanning AI-specific and traditional software domains, yet current research addresses these separately. This study bridges this gap through comparative evaluation of Function Calling architecture and Model Context Protocol (MCP) deployment paradigms using a unified threat classification framework. We tested 3,250 attack scenarios across seven language models, evaluating simple, composed, and chained attacks targeting both AI-specific threats (prompt injection) and software vulnerabilities (JSON injection, denial-of-service). Function Calling showed higher overall attack success rates (73.5% vs 62.59% for MCP), with greater system-centric vulnerability while MCP exhibited increased LLM-centric exposure. Attack complexity dramatically amplified effectiveness, with chained attacks achieving 91-96% success rates. Counterintuitively, advanced reasoning models demonstrated higher exploitability despite better threat detection. Results demonstrate that architectural choices fundamentally reshape threat landscapes. This work establishes methodological foundations for cross-domain LLM agent security assessment and provides evidence-based guidance for secure deployment. Code and experimental materials are available at https: // github. com/ theconsciouslab-ai/llm-agent-security."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06323","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:27:28Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:27:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":91,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3a%2810.48550%2farXiv.2507.06306+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06310+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06323+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06335+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06376+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06378+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06396+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06419+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06427+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06445+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06448+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06449+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06461+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06466+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06482+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06484+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06485+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06489+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06510+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06516+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06523+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06526+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06528+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06529+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06547+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06554+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06564+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06565+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06573+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06590+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06603+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06607+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06613+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06622+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06623+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06651+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06656+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06674+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06679+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06710+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06722+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2